window.__courseChapter("v2-15", {"v2-15-00-overview": {"title": "Overview: the big ideas", "summary": "The chapter's key concepts and how its sections fit together.", "source": {"chapter": "v2-15", "sections": ["Purpose", "concept map"]}, "blocks": [{"type": "text", "md": "Welcome to **Sustainable AI** (Vol II). This overview gives you the chapter's big ideas before the section-by-section lessons. Read the claims once, flip the flashcards, then take the warm-up quiz: it is fine to miss questions here, the lessons that follow fill the gaps."}, {"type": "from_book", "source": "Vol II, Sustainable AI, Purpose", "md": "Power is an operational expense and a physical constraint on what can be built. A data center has a power budget determined by electrical infrastructure and cooling capacity; a training run that exceeds it cannot proceed at the intended scale regardless of its financial budget. Adding accelerators only helps if the site can supply their electricity and remove their heat. A design that runs efficiently on one machine can therefore become infeasible when replicated across a fleet."}, {"type": "flow", "title": "The path through this chapter", "nodes": [{"label": "The Energy Ceiling", "detail": "Lesson 1 covers this section."}, {"label": "The Scale of Environmental Impact", "detail": "Lesson 2 covers this section."}, {"label": "Exponential Growth vs. Physical Constrai", "detail": "Lesson 3 covers this section."}, {"label": "Energy Measurement and Modeling", "detail": "Lesson 4 covers this section."}, {"label": "Carbon Footprint Calculation", "detail": "Lesson 5 covers this section."}, {"label": "Data Center Energy and Resource Consumpt", "detail": "Lesson 6 covers this section."}, {"label": "Training vs. Inference Energy Analysis", "detail": "Lesson 7 covers this section."}]}, {"type": "table", "headers": ["Concept", "The claim to remember"], "rows": [["**Power ceiling versus energy account**", "A facility cannot draw more than its grid feed and cooling capacity at any instant, while run energy integrates average power over time, so a short run can be infeasible at modest energy and a long run feasible at large energy."], ["**Sustainability measurement contract**", "An energy or carbon number supports a comparison only when it extends the reproducible measurement contract with five fields, the functional unit, system boundary, attribution rule, carbon signal, and time and location of operation."], ["**Operational carbon chain**", "Operational CO2 = IT energy x PUE x grid carbon intensity, three terms acting at separate tiers, and PUE measures only non-IT overhead, so a coal-fed site can beat a hydro-fed one on PUE."], ["**Embodied carbon allocation**", "Manufacturing emissions are fixed before first power-on and allocated to work by a declared rule, so their share of a job's footprint rises on clean grids and at low utilization and decides when replacing hardware pays."], ["**Dominant lifecycle carbon term**", "Lifecycle CO2 is training plus inference plus embodied, where development multiplies training and inference recurs with query volume and idle capacity, so for widely served models inference is usually the dominant term and picks the lever."], ["**Carbon-aware placement and time-shifting**", "Moving work to cleaner regions or hours changes the emissions factor, not the energy, so it must use a marginal signal from a forecast, meet deadline, data, capacity, and reliability constraints, and beat the cost of the move."], ["**Water accounting**", "Evaporative heat rejection lowers facility energy at the price of water, so water is a separate, location-weighted account (WUE at the IT boundary, withdrawal vs consumption, direct vs indirect) traded against energy."], ["**Demand outruns efficiency**", "Fleet energy is energy per job times number of jobs, and demand outruns efficiency twice, through scaling laws that price each loss reduction in multiplicative compute and through rebound, so efficiency targets need absolute budgets."], ["**Facility overhead boundary**", "PUE is facility energy over IT energy, defined once at the facility boundary, so budgeting by accelerator TDP underestimates load and the boundary decides where each loss is counted: upstream transformer, UPS, and distribution losses raise PUE while supply and regulator losses stay in IT load."], ["**Energy crossover versus performance ridge**", "Modeled energy crosses over from movement-dominated to arithmetic-dominated at an intensity set by energy per byte over energy per operation, a different point from the performance ridge, so batch-1 decode spends most of its energy moving bytes."], ["**Edge and federated energy accounting**", "Local execution saves energy only when the radio, remote compute, and transfers it avoids exceed the device's added compute, idle power, and manufacturing, so edge and federated deployment move energy rather than remove it."]]}, {"type": "callout", "kind": "key", "title": "Formulas you will meet", "md": "$$P_{\\text{total}} = P_{\\text{dynamic}} + P_{\\text{static}} = \\alpha C V^2 f + V I_{\\text{leak}}$$\n\n$$\\text{PUE} = \\frac{P_{\\text{total\\_facility}}}{P_{\\text{IT\\_equipment}}}$$\n\n$$\\text{WUE} = \\frac{W_{\\text{annual\\_water\\_usage}}}{P_{\\text{IT\\_equipment\\_energy}}}$$\n\n$$\\text{AI} = \\frac{\\text{Total FLOPs}}{\\text{Total Bytes Moved}}$$"}, {"type": "flashcards", "id": "v2-15-cards-concepts", "title": "Key concepts", "cards": [{"front": "Power ceiling versus energy account", "back": "A facility cannot draw more than its grid feed and cooling capacity at any instant, while run energy integrates average power over time, so a short run can be infeasible at modest energy and a long run feasible at large energy."}, {"front": "Sustainability measurement contract", "back": "An energy or carbon number supports a comparison only when it extends the reproducible measurement contract with five fields, the functional unit, system boundary, attribution rule, carbon signal, and time and location of operation."}, {"front": "Operational carbon chain", "back": "Operational CO2 = IT energy x PUE x grid carbon intensity, three terms acting at separate tiers, and PUE measures only non-IT overhead, so a coal-fed site can beat a hydro-fed one on PUE."}, {"front": "Embodied carbon allocation", "back": "Manufacturing emissions are fixed before first power-on and allocated to work by a declared rule, so their share of a job's footprint rises on clean grids and at low utilization and decides when replacing hardware pays."}, {"front": "Dominant lifecycle carbon term", "back": "Lifecycle CO2 is training plus inference plus embodied, where development multiplies training and inference recurs with query volume and idle capacity, so for widely served models inference is usually the dominant term and picks the lever."}, {"front": "Carbon-aware placement and time-shifting", "back": "Moving work to cleaner regions or hours changes the emissions factor, not the energy, so it must use a marginal signal from a forecast, meet deadline, data, capacity, and reliability constraints, and beat the cost of the move."}, {"front": "Water accounting", "back": "Evaporative heat rejection lowers facility energy at the price of water, so water is a separate, location-weighted account (WUE at the IT boundary, withdrawal vs consumption, direct vs indirect) traded against energy."}, {"front": "Demand outruns efficiency", "back": "Fleet energy is energy per job times number of jobs, and demand outruns efficiency twice, through scaling laws that price each loss reduction in multiplicative compute and through rebound, so efficiency targets need absolute budgets."}, {"front": "Facility overhead boundary", "back": "PUE is facility energy over IT energy, defined once at the facility boundary, so budgeting by accelerator TDP underestimates load and the boundary decides where each loss is counted: upstream transformer, UPS, and distribution losses raise PUE while supply and regulator losses stay in IT load."}, {"front": "Energy crossover versus performance ridge", "back": "Modeled energy crosses over from movement-dominated to arithmetic-dominated at an intensity set by energy per byte over energy per operation, a different point from the performance ridge, so batch-1 decode spends most of its energy moving bytes."}]}, {"type": "quiz", "id": "v2-15-quiz-warmup", "title": "Warm-up: match the big ideas", "questions": [{"type": "single", "q": "Which statement captures **Power ceiling versus energy account**?", "options": ["Modeled energy crosses over from movement-dominated to arithmetic-dominated at an intensity set by energy per byte over energy per operation, a different point from the performance ridge, so batch-1 decode spends most of its energy moving bytes.", "A facility cannot draw more than its grid feed and cooling capacity at any instant, while run energy integrates average power over time, so a short run can be infeasible at modest energy and a long run feasible at large energy.", "Lifecycle CO2 is training plus inference plus embodied, where development multiplies training and inference recurs with query volume and idle capacity, so for widely served models inference is usually the dominant term and picks the lever.", "An energy or carbon number supports a comparison only when it extends the reproducible measurement contract with five fields, the functional unit, system boundary, attribution rule, carbon signal, and time and location of operation."], "answer": 1, "explanation": "**Power ceiling versus energy account:** A facility cannot draw more than its grid feed and cooling capacity at any instant, while run energy integrates average power over time, so a short run can be infeasible at modest energy and a long run feasible at large energy.", "hints": ["Look for the statement whose subject is Power ceiling versus energy account itself, not a neighbouring idea."]}, {"type": "single", "q": "Which statement captures **Sustainability measurement contract**?", "options": ["Moving work to cleaner regions or hours changes the emissions factor, not the energy, so it must use a marginal signal from a forecast, meet deadline, data, capacity, and reliability constraints, and beat the cost of the move.", "Manufacturing emissions are fixed before first power-on and allocated to work by a declared rule, so their share of a job's footprint rises on clean grids and at low utilization and decides when replacing hardware pays.", "An energy or carbon number supports a comparison only when it extends the reproducible measurement contract with five fields, the functional unit, system boundary, attribution rule, carbon signal, and time and location of operation.", "Operational CO2 = IT energy x PUE x grid carbon intensity, three terms acting at separate tiers, and PUE measures only non-IT overhead, so a coal-fed site can beat a hydro-fed one on PUE."], "answer": 2, "explanation": "**Sustainability measurement contract:** An energy or carbon number supports a comparison only when it extends the reproducible measurement contract with five fields, the functional unit, system boundary, attribution rule, carbon signal, and time and location of operation.", "hints": ["Look for the statement whose subject is Sustainability measurement contract itself, not a neighbouring idea."]}, {"type": "single", "q": "Which statement captures **Operational carbon chain**?", "options": ["Operational CO2 = IT energy x PUE x grid carbon intensity, three terms acting at separate tiers, and PUE measures only non-IT overhead, so a coal-fed site can beat a hydro-fed one on PUE.", "Evaporative heat rejection lowers facility energy at the price of water, so water is a separate, location-weighted account (WUE at the IT boundary, withdrawal vs consumption, direct vs indirect) traded against energy.", "Moving work to cleaner regions or hours changes the emissions factor, not the energy, so it must use a marginal signal from a forecast, meet deadline, data, capacity, and reliability constraints, and beat the cost of the move.", "Local execution saves energy only when the radio, remote compute, and transfers it avoids exceed the device's added compute, idle power, and manufacturing, so edge and federated deployment move energy rather than remove it."], "answer": 0, "explanation": "**Operational carbon chain:** Operational CO2 = IT energy x PUE x grid carbon intensity, three terms acting at separate tiers, and PUE measures only non-IT overhead, so a coal-fed site can beat a hydro-fed one on PUE.", "hints": ["Look for the statement whose subject is Operational carbon chain itself, not a neighbouring idea."]}, {"type": "single", "q": "Which statement captures **Embodied carbon allocation**?", "options": ["Modeled energy crosses over from movement-dominated to arithmetic-dominated at an intensity set by energy per byte over energy per operation, a different point from the performance ridge, so batch-1 decode spends most of its energy moving bytes.", "Evaporative heat rejection lowers facility energy at the price of water, so water is a separate, location-weighted account (WUE at the IT boundary, withdrawal vs consumption, direct vs indirect) traded against energy.", "An energy or carbon number supports a comparison only when it extends the reproducible measurement contract with five fields, the functional unit, system boundary, attribution rule, carbon signal, and time and location of operation.", "Manufacturing emissions are fixed before first power-on and allocated to work by a declared rule, so their share of a job's footprint rises on clean grids and at low utilization and decides when replacing hardware pays."], "answer": 3, "explanation": "**Embodied carbon allocation:** Manufacturing emissions are fixed before first power-on and allocated to work by a declared rule, so their share of a job's footprint rises on clean grids and at low utilization and decides when replacing hardware pays.", "hints": ["Look for the statement whose subject is Embodied carbon allocation itself, not a neighbouring idea."]}, {"type": "single", "q": "Which statement captures **Dominant lifecycle carbon term**?", "options": ["Manufacturing emissions are fixed before first power-on and allocated to work by a declared rule, so their share of a job's footprint rises on clean grids and at low utilization and decides when replacing hardware pays.", "Lifecycle CO2 is training plus inference plus embodied, where development multiplies training and inference recurs with query volume and idle capacity, so for widely served models inference is usually the dominant term and picks the lever.", "Local execution saves energy only when the radio, remote compute, and transfers it avoids exceed the device's added compute, idle power, and manufacturing, so edge and federated deployment move energy rather than remove it.", "Fleet energy is energy per job times number of jobs, and demand outruns efficiency twice, through scaling laws that price each loss reduction in multiplicative compute and through rebound, so efficiency targets need absolute budgets."], "answer": 1, "explanation": "**Dominant lifecycle carbon term:** Lifecycle CO2 is training plus inference plus embodied, where development multiplies training and inference recurs with query volume and idle capacity, so for widely served models inference is usually the dominant term and picks the lever.", "hints": ["Look for the statement whose subject is Dominant lifecycle carbon term itself, not a neighbouring idea."]}]}, {"type": "summary", "points": ["Power ceiling versus energy account: A facility cannot draw more than its grid feed and cooling capacity at any instant, while run energy integrates average power over time, so a short run can be infeasible at modest energy and a long run feasible at large energy.", "Sustainability measurement contract: An energy or carbon number supports a comparison only when it extends the reproducible measurement contract with five fields, the functional unit, system boundary, attribution rule, carbon signal, and time and location of operation.", "Operational carbon chain: Operational CO2 = IT energy x PUE x grid carbon intensity, three terms acting at separate tiers, and PUE measures only non-IT overhead, so a coal-fed site can beat a hydro-fed one on PUE.", "Embodied carbon allocation: Manufacturing emissions are fixed before first power-on and allocated to work by a declared rule, so their share of a job's footprint rises on clean grids and at low utilization and decides when replacing hardware pays."]}], "terms": [{"term": "Power ceiling versus energy account", "def": "A facility cannot draw more than its grid feed and cooling capacity at any instant, while run energy integrates average power over time, so a short run can be infeasible at modest energy and a long run feasible at large energy."}, {"term": "Sustainability measurement contract", "def": "An energy or carbon number supports a comparison only when it extends the reproducible measurement contract with five fields, the functional unit, system boundary, attribution rule, carbon signal, and time and location of operation."}, {"term": "Operational carbon chain", "def": "Operational CO2 = IT energy x PUE x grid carbon intensity, three terms acting at separate tiers, and PUE measures only non-IT overhead, so a coal-fed site can beat a hydro-fed one on PUE."}, {"term": "Embodied carbon allocation", "def": "Manufacturing emissions are fixed before first power-on and allocated to work by a declared rule, so their share of a job's footprint rises on clean grids and at low utilization and decides when replacing hardware pays."}, {"term": "Dominant lifecycle carbon term", "def": "Lifecycle CO2 is training plus inference plus embodied, where development multiplies training and inference recurs with query volume and idle capacity, so for widely served models inference is usually the dominant term and picks the lever."}, {"term": "Carbon-aware placement and time-shifting", "def": "Moving work to cleaner regions or hours changes the emissions factor, not the energy, so it must use a marginal signal from a forecast, meet deadline, data, capacity, and reliability constraints, and beat the cost of the move."}, {"term": "Water accounting", "def": "Evaporative heat rejection lowers facility energy at the price of water, so water is a separate, location-weighted account (WUE at the IT boundary, withdrawal vs consumption, direct vs indirect) traded against energy."}, {"term": "Demand outruns efficiency", "def": "Fleet energy is energy per job times number of jobs, and demand outruns efficiency twice, through scaling laws that price each loss reduction in multiplicative compute and through rebound, so efficiency targets need absolute budgets."}, {"term": "Facility overhead boundary", "def": "PUE is facility energy over IT energy, defined once at the facility boundary, so budgeting by accelerator TDP underestimates load and the boundary decides where each loss is counted: upstream transformer, UPS, and distribution losses raise PUE while supply and regulator losses stay in IT load."}, {"term": "Energy crossover versus performance ridge", "def": "Modeled energy crosses over from movement-dominated to arithmetic-dominated at an intensity set by energy per byte over energy per operation, a different point from the performance ridge, so batch-1 decode spends most of its energy moving bytes."}, {"term": "Edge and federated energy accounting", "def": "Local execution saves energy only when the radio, remote compute, and transfers it avoids exceed the device's added compute, idle power, and manufacturing, so edge and federated deployment move energy rather than remove it."}], "id": "v2-15-00-overview", "chapter": "v2-15", "kind": "lesson", "minutes": 10}, "v2-15-01-the-energy-ceiling": {"title": "The Energy Ceiling", "summary": "Classify energy and power delivery as physical system constraints rather than financial or reporting concerns.", "source": {"chapter": "v2-15", "sections": ["The Energy Ceiling"]}, "blocks": [{"type": "text", "md": "By the end of this lesson you should be able to:\n\n- Classify energy and power delivery as physical system constraints rather than financial or reporting concerns\n- Calculate scenario emissions and compare feasible placement and algorithmic interventions under a declared accounting boundary\n- Evaluate the claim that hardware efficiency scaling alone can offset exponential growth in AI compute demand\n- Explain how energy storage buffers synchronized training load changes and identify the measurements needed to size it"}, {"type": "from_book", "source": "Vol II, Sustainable AI, § The Energy Ceiling", "md": "A model can be secure against adversaries and robust under distribution shift yet still fail if the available grid cannot power its accelerators or the facility cannot cool them. **Sustainability** begins at that boundary, where accuracy, latency, and reliability are no longer enough because the system must also fit within power, cooling, water, carbon, and hardware lifetime budgets. Every operation a model performs is paid for in energy, and power density sets the ceiling on data-center computational capacity."}, {"type": "callout", "kind": "note", "title": "What the section covers", "md": "Pay attention to: sustainability as a physical ceiling rather than a reporting concern; geographic carbon intensity arithmetic; hardware efficiency gains versus compute growth gap; grid transient dynamics during synchronized distributed training."}, {"type": "reveal", "prompt": "Think first: A research team plans a 10,000 MWh training run, measured as facility electricity. Quebec and Poland both meet its capacity, data-access, quality, and deadline requirements, and transfer overhead is negligible. The stated grid factors are approximately 20 and 820 grams of CO2 per kWh, respectively, on a common boundary. An algorithmic change would reduce facility energy in Poland by 15 percent at unchanged quality. Calculate the emissions under these alternatives and justify which offers the larger reduction in this scenario.", "md": "At the supplied factors, Quebec emits 200 tonnes of CO2 and Poland emits 8,200 tonnes. The algorithmic change in Poland lowers that result to 6,970 tonnes, so Quebec offers the larger reduction among these alternatives. This conclusion depends on the stated equal-energy baseline and feasible placements; a regional intensity ratio alone does not rank unmeasured interventions or establish the effect of shifting actual grid demand."}, {"type": "quiz", "id": "v2-15-01-quiz", "title": "Check your understanding", "questions": [{"q": "A hyperscaler plans a 500 MW campus for a new foundation model cluster, but the local utility interconnect approval is capped at 320 MW for the next three years. The organization's capital reserves could cover the electricity cost multiple times over. Which framing best captures why sustainable AI treats energy as a first-class engineering constraint rather than a financial or reporting concern?", "explanation": "The correct answer is D. Power delivery and grid interconnect capacity impose a hard physical ceiling that capital cannot bypass on deployment timescales, making power an operational constraint that model architectures and cluster sizing must fit within. The chapter's central thesis is that sustainability is a physical capacity ceiling, not a financial or reporting line item: a 500 MW cluster facing a 320 MW interconnect cannot operate at planned scale regardless of capital expenditure. An administrative-reporting framing mistakes accounting disclosures for physical bounds; a financial-hedging framing treats capacity as a price risk rather than a physical limit; and a voluntary-offset framing ignores physical grid interconnect constraints.", "type": "single", "options": ["Carbon accounting frameworks mandate disclosing total planned capacity upfront, creating an administrative filing hurdle before training begins.", "Electricity price fluctuations create budget volatility, so the primary engineering intervention is financial hedging.", "Corporate sustainability reporting requires purchasing voluntary offsets for unapproved megawatts, adding financial cost without altering physical infrastructure.", "Power delivery and grid interconnect capacity impose a hard physical ceiling that capital cannot bypass on deployment timescales, making power an operational constraint that model architectures and cluster sizing must fit within."], "answer": 3, "hints": ["Think about: classify energy and power delivery as physical system constraints rather than financial or reporting concerns"]}, {"q": "Because successive generations of specialized AI accelerators deliver substantial improvements in energy efficiency (FLOP/s per watt), organizations can rely on hardware innovation alone to keep total data center power consumption flat while scaling model parameters.", "explanation": "False. Hardware improvements alone do not guarantee flat fleet power. Total energy depends on workload volume and realized work per joule at a consistent boundary; power also depends on the execution schedule. A historical compute-growth curve does not determine either quantity without those measurements.", "type": "truefalse", "answer": false, "hints": ["Think about: evaluate the claim that hardware efficiency scaling alone can offset exponential growth in AI compute demand"]}]}, {"type": "reveal", "prompt": "Explain it: Thousands of GPUs alternate between dense computation and collective communication, producing correlated changes in device power. Explain how bidirectional energy storage can buffer the change at a protected electrical boundary, and what measurements determine its timing and capacity.", "md": "When load falls, storage can absorb surplus input; when load rises, it can discharge to cover the deficit while upstream supply responds. The controller must follow the measured load and supply response at the protected boundary, including losses, state of charge, and power limits. Device-side changes need not propagate unchanged to the grid, and buffering can act during a transition rather than only after it."}, {"type": "summary", "points": ["You can now classify energy and power delivery as physical system constraints rather than financial or reporting concerns.", "You can now calculate scenario emissions and compare feasible placement and algorithmic interventions under a declared accounting boundary.", "You can now evaluate the claim that hardware efficiency scaling alone can offset exponential growth in AI compute demand.", "You can now explain how energy storage buffers synchronized training load changes and identify the measurements needed to size it."]}], "id": "v2-15-01-the-energy-ceiling", "chapter": "v2-15", "kind": "lesson", "minutes": 5}, "v2-15-02-the-scale-of-environmental-impac": {"title": "The Scale of Environmental Impact", "summary": "Calculate attributed training emissions from facility energy and grid factor, and identify what a placement comparison does and does not establish.", "source": {"chapter": "v2-15", "sections": ["The Scale of Environmental Impact"]}, "blocks": [{"type": "text", "md": "By the end of this lesson you should be able to:\n\n- Calculate attributed training emissions from facility energy and grid factor, and identify what a placement comparison does and does not establish\n- Distinguish growth in training compute from growth in training energy\n- Identify the environmental costs that an electricity-only footprint omits\n- Explain why geographic and temporal carbon levers must be evaluated jointly rather than multiplied"}, {"type": "from_book", "source": "Vol II, Sustainable AI, § The Scale of Environmental Impact", "md": "A training run measured in megawatt-hours is hard to reason about, because few engineers carry an intuition for what a megawatt-hour of grid electricity costs the atmosphere. The introduction quoted GPT-3's training emissions as one of the costs that scaling imposes (a later section). The derivation behind that figure converts the run's energy into emissions with a grid factor and then into a familiar anchor, the CO~2~ of a trans-Atlantic flight, which turns an abstract number into one with physical stakes."}, {"type": "callout", "kind": "note", "title": "What the section covers", "md": "Pay attention to: Converting facility energy into attributed emissions with a grid factor; Geographic placement as a grid-factor lever at equal facility energy; Compute growth versus energy growth; Embodied manufacturing impact and burdens on host communities."}, {"type": "reveal", "prompt": "Think first: A Quebec-versus-Poland comparison shows a 41-fold emissions difference for the same 10,000 MWh run, and carbon-aware scheduling can also shift work to cleaner hours. Explain why the geographic and temporal gains cannot simply be multiplied, and how the decision should be evaluated.", "md": "Both levers act on the same quantity, the emissions factor of the electricity that supplies a fixed amount of energy, and the 41-fold ratio already uses annual-average factors at equal facility energy. At a hydro-dominated site the hourly factor stays close to its low average, so waiting for cleaner hours there leaves little additional gain. The placement and the schedule must therefore be evaluated as one decision, using the hourly or marginal factors the schedule would actually see along with the job's power, deadline, and capacity constraints."}, {"type": "quiz", "id": "v2-15-02-quiz", "title": "Check your understanding", "questions": [{"q": "A 2,000 MWh training run can be placed at a site whose annual-average grid factor is 400 g CO~2~ per kWh or at one whose factor is 20 g CO~2~ per kWh, with facility electricity held equal. Which conclusion follows?", "explanation": "The correct answer is B. Emissions equal facility energy times the grid factor, so 2,000,000 kWh gives about 800 tonnes at 400 g per kWh and about 40 tonnes at 20 g per kWh. The grid factor changes attributed emissions, not the energy the run consumes. A placement comparison isolates one factor and cannot rank an unmeasured algorithmic change, and a temporal gain cannot simply be multiplied onto an annual-average placement gain.", "type": "single", "options": ["The cleaner site also cuts the run's energy twentyfold, because low-carbon grids deliver electricity more efficiently.", "Attributed emissions fall from about 800 tonnes to about 40 tonnes, a twentyfold difference that isolates the grid factor at equal facility energy.", "This comparison alone ranks placement against an algorithmic change, because both reduce emissions.", "Waiting for cleaner hours at the cleaner site would multiply the saving by a further twentyfold."], "answer": 1, "hints": ["Think about: calculate attributed training emissions from facility energy and grid factor, and identify what a placement comparison does and does not establish."]}, {"q": "Because training compute for notable models has grown by many orders of magnitude, training energy has grown by the same multiplier.", "explanation": "False. A compute multiplier becomes an energy multiplier only after dividing by realized work per joule at a stated boundary. Efficiency gains in hardware and execution can make energy grow more slowly than compute, so the energy trend must be measured rather than read off a compute curve.", "type": "truefalse", "answer": false, "hints": ["Think about: distinguish growth in training compute from growth in training energy."]}, {"q": "An operator reports its AI fleet's environmental footprint as metered electricity multiplied by the grid factor. What does this account leave out?", "explanation": "The correct answer is D. Producing processors carries embodied carbon and consumes water, gases, and chemicals before any job runs, and hosting a fleet assigns land, water, grid capacity, and disposal burdens to communities. Grid factors describe electricity supply, not manufacturing. Retirement does not necessarily create waste, because equipment can be redeployed, resold, or recycled, so disposal must be estimated from the actual end-of-life route.", "type": "single", "options": ["Nothing, because operational electricity is the only environmental cost of AI hardware.", "Only facility overhead, because grid factors already include the cost of manufacturing.", "A fixed disposal charge per device, because retiring any accelerator necessarily creates e-waste.", "Manufacturing impacts that precede the first training run, such as embodied carbon, ultrapure water, and specialty gases, and the land, water, and grid capacity that host communities supply."], "answer": 3, "hints": ["Think about: identify the environmental costs that an electricity-only footprint omits."]}]}, {"type": "summary", "points": ["You can now calculate attributed training emissions from facility energy and grid factor, and identify what a placement comparison does and does not establish.", "You can now distinguish growth in training compute from growth in training energy.", "You can now identify the environmental costs that an electricity-only footprint omits.", "You can now explain why geographic and temporal carbon levers must be evaluated jointly rather than multiplied."]}], "id": "v2-15-02-the-scale-of-environmental-impac", "chapter": "v2-15", "kind": "lesson", "minutes": 5}, "v2-15-03-exponential-growth-vs-physical-c": {"title": "Exponential Growth vs. Physical Constraints", "summary": "Calculate fleet energy under rebound and distinguish rebound from scaling-driven demand growth.", "source": {"chapter": "v2-15", "sections": ["Exponential Growth vs. Physical Constraints", "Power envelopes and the energy wall", "Data center grid dynamics", "Training-scale energy concentration"]}, "blocks": [{"type": "text", "md": "By the end of this lesson you should be able to:\n\n- Calculate fleet energy under rebound and distinguish rebound from scaling-driven demand growth\n- Distinguish the instantaneous power envelope from the cumulative energy account when judging a training plan's feasibility\n- Identify the grid-interface mechanism that absorbs correlated training load swings and state what it does and does not change\n- Explain why temporal concentration, not annual energy, sets the facility and grid requirements of a training campaign\n- Analyze how the scaling-law compute exponent translates successive quality gains into multiplicative training energy"}, {"type": "from_book", "source": "Vol II, Sustainable AI, § Exponential Growth vs. Physical Constraints", "md": "Between … and …, the training compute of frontier models expanded from roughly … FLOPs for AlexNet to … for GPT-4-class systems, an increase of about … million times in … years that outpaced hardware efficiency gains by orders of magnitude. @sevilla2022 estimate that the training compute of notable machine learning models doubled roughly every six months after 2010, far faster than the two-year cadence historically associated with transistor scaling."}, {"type": "callout", "kind": "note", "title": "What the section covers", "md": "In the book this section runs through:\n\n- Power envelopes and the energy wall\n- Data center grid dynamics\n- Training-scale energy concentration\n\nPay attention to: Fleet energy as energy per task times task volume, and rebound; Instantaneous power limits versus cumulative energy accounts; Energy buffering at the grid interface; Temporal concentration of training load."}, {"type": "reveal", "prompt": "Think first: In the regime studied by Kaplan et al., each 10 percent reduction in compute-limited loss costs about 8.2 times the compute. Explain what two successive 10 percent reductions imply for training energy, and what would have to hold for energy not to grow by the same factor.", "md": "The multiples compound, so two successive 10 percent reductions cost about 8.2 times 8.2, or roughly 67 times, the original compute, because the small compute exponent makes each fixed fractional loss reduction a multiplicative increase in work. Training energy grows by the same factor unless realized FLOPs per joule improve at the same pace, which historical hardware gains have not matched. The diminishing return of the scaling law therefore reaches the facility as a multiplicative energy demand, which is why compute demand has outrun hardware efficiency."}, {"type": "quiz", "id": "v2-15-03-quiz", "title": "Check your understanding", "questions": [{"q": "An inference optimization cuts energy per task by a factor of 4, and the lower cost raises task volume by a factor of 20, with the workload mix unchanged. What happens to fleet energy?", "explanation": "The correct answer is C. Fleet energy is energy per completed task times the number of completed tasks, so one quarter of the energy times 20 times the volume gives five times the energy. Reading only the per-task gain misses the demand response, and multiplying both factors as growth treats the efficiency gain as a cost. This rebound differs from scaling-driven growth, which raises the compute each frontier model requires.", "type": "single", "options": ["It falls by a factor of 4, because per-task efficiency sets fleet energy.", "It stays the same, because efficiency and demand cancel.", "It rises by a factor of 5, because fleet energy is energy per task multiplied by the number of tasks.", "It rises by a factor of 80, because the efficiency gain and the demand growth compound."], "answer": 2, "hints": ["Think about: calculate fleet energy under rebound and distinguish rebound from scaling-driven demand growth."]}, {"q": "A team plans a three-day run that needs 120 MW of accelerator power at once in a facility with an 80 MW grid feed. They argue the run fits because its total energy is less than the facility uses in a month. What is wrong with the argument?", "explanation": "The correct answer is A. Facility power cannot exceed the grid feed at any instant, which caps active servers whatever the job's duration, while energy integrates average power over time and enters the electricity and emissions accounts. A modest energy total therefore cannot license a power draw above the feed. Treating the limits as interchangeable confuses the two physical constraints, and the tariff and PUE answers address the energy account rather than the power cap.", "type": "single", "options": ["The power limit is instantaneous, so the feed caps how many servers can be active at once regardless of run length; the plan must lower concurrency and accept a longer run, or obtain more deliverable power.", "Nothing, because over a month an energy budget and a power cap are interchangeable.", "The run fails only on cost, because energy drawn above the feed is billed at a peak tariff.", "The only error is omitting PUE; adding facility overhead would show that the run's energy exceeds the monthly budget."], "answer": 0, "hints": ["Think about: distinguish the instantaneous power envelope from the cumulative energy account when judging a training plan's feasibility."]}, {"q": "Synchronized training phases swing accelerator draw together across thousands of devices, and a battery or UPS stage that supplies or absorbs energy during each load change provides energy ____, whose location, capacity, and control response must match the electrical boundary being protected.", "explanation": "buffering. Energy buffering smooths the load step that coordinated training phases would otherwise push toward the utility connection; it protects a specific electrical boundary rather than reducing the run's total energy.", "type": "fill", "answer": ["buffering"], "hints": ["Think about: identify the grid-interface mechanism that absorbs correlated training load swings and state what it does and does not change."]}, {"q": "Because a frontier training run's yearly energy is a modest share of a region's consumption, the facility can be provisioned for that run's annual-average power.", "explanation": "False. A training run concentrates its draw into weeks or months of sustained peak, and synchronized phases swing that draw together across the cluster. The facility must provision for the sustained peak, and the grid sees one large correlated load rather than many small independent ones.", "type": "truefalse", "answer": false, "hints": ["Think about: explain why temporal concentration, not annual energy, sets the facility and grid requirements of a training campaign."]}]}, {"type": "summary", "points": ["You can now calculate fleet energy under rebound and distinguish rebound from scaling-driven demand growth.", "You can now distinguish the instantaneous power envelope from the cumulative energy account when judging a training plan's feasibility.", "You can now identify the grid-interface mechanism that absorbs correlated training load swings and state what it does and does not change.", "You can now explain why temporal concentration, not annual energy, sets the facility and grid requirements of a training campaign."]}], "id": "v2-15-03-exponential-growth-vs-physical-c", "chapter": "v2-15", "kind": "lesson", "minutes": 5}, "v2-15-04-energy-measurement-and-modeling": {"title": "Energy Measurement and Modeling", "summary": "Apply the energy-roofline crossover to classify a kernel's dominant energy component and select the corresponding optimization.", "source": {"chapter": "v2-15", "sections": ["Energy Measurement and Modeling", "Power modeling fundamentals", "Systematic energy metrics", "Energy measurement techniques"]}, "blocks": [{"type": "text", "md": "By the end of this lesson you should be able to:\n\n- Apply the energy-roofline crossover to classify a kernel's dominant energy component and select the corresponding optimization\n- Calculate annual facility energy savings resulting from PUE improvements and justify infrastructure optimization as a high-leverage sustainability intervention\n- Explain the role of the switching activity factor in the CMOS dynamic power equation\n- Select instrumentation capable of measuring the declared duty cycle and electrical boundary of a TinyML system\n- Evaluate the requirement of incorporating facility overhead (PUE) into comprehensive AI energy accounting"}, {"type": "from_book", "source": "Vol II, Sustainable AI, § Energy Measurement and Modeling", "md": "A cluster drawing five megawatts during a large language model training run directs only a fraction of that power into matrix multiplications; host processors, memory, networking, power conversion, and cooling consume the rest. Engineers cannot optimize those losses from a single monthly utility bill."}, {"type": "callout", "kind": "note", "title": "What the section covers", "md": "In the book this section runs through:\n\n- Power modeling fundamentals\n- Systematic energy metrics\n- Energy measurement techniques\n\nPay attention to: CMOS power decomposition and switching activity; energy roofline model and crossover intensity; PUE, WUE, and CUE facility metrics; edge vs datacenter measurement telemetry."}, {"type": "reveal", "prompt": "Think first: A 2 MW IT load cluster improves its Power Usage Effectiveness (PUE) from 1.58 to 1.10 without altering any model weights or server SKUs. Calculate the annual electricity savings in megawatt-hours (MWh) assuming 24/7 continuous operation, and explain why facility optimization is considered a first-order sustainability lever.", "md": "At PUE = 1.58, total facility power is \\$2 MW × 1.58 = 3.16 MW. At PUE = 1.10, total power drops to \\$2 MW × 1.10 = 2.20 MW, saving \\$0.96 MW continuously. Over one year (\\$8,760 hours), this saves \\$0.96 MW × 8,760 h approx 8,410 MWh. This demonstrates that facility infrastructure optimization delivers multi-gigawatt-hour savings equivalent to massive algorithmic optimizations without requiring changes to model code or accuracy."}, {"type": "quiz", "id": "v2-15-04-quiz", "title": "Check your understanding", "questions": [{"q": "An attention kernel has an arithmetic intensity of 3 FLOP/byte. An illustrative chip-and-memory energy model assigns 10 pJ per FLOP and 160 pJ per byte moved from DRAM, excluding static and facility energy. Which intervention directly targets the larger modeled energy contribution?", "explanation": "The correct answer is C. The crossover is 16 FLOP/byte, so at 3 FLOP/byte movement contributes more energy than arithmetic in this model. Fusion and tiling can reduce that traffic when implemented successfully. Peak throughput alone establishes neither an energy saving nor the execution bottleneck; PUE does not eliminate memory stalls. Measure the complete workload to confirm the saving.", "type": "single", "options": ["Replacing the accelerator with one offering double the peak TFLOP/s while retaining the same memory bandwidth and memory technology.", "Increasing arithmetic precision from FP16 to FP32 to execute more FLOPs per memory transaction.", "Fusing normalization and attention operators and tiling intermediate activations to keep data in on-chip SRAM, reducing DRAM traffic.", "Lowering facility PUE from 1.5 to 1.1 to eliminate memory stall cycles."], "answer": 2, "hints": ["Think about: apply the energy-roofline crossover to classify a kernel's dominant energy component and select the corresponding optimization"]}, {"q": "In the CMOS dynamic power equation $P_{\\text{dynamic}}=\\alpha_{\\text{sw}}CV^2f$, the parameter $\\alpha_{\\text{sw}}$ is the `____`, summarizing capacitance-weighted switching per clock cycle under the chosen counting convention.", "explanation": "switching activity factor. It scales dynamic power linearly at fixed capacitance, voltage, and frequency; more switching is not necessarily more useful computation or greater energy efficiency.", "type": "fill", "answer": ["switching activity factor"], "hints": ["Think about: explain the role of the switching activity factor in the CMOS dynamic power equation"]}, {"q": "An engineer needs to profile the energy consumption of a sub-watt microcontroller executing an on-device wake-word detection model that spends 98 percent of its time in deep sleep drawing 30 $\\mu\\text{W}$. The microcontroller lacks on-chip energy counters. Which measurement approach is appropriate?", "explanation": "The correct answer is D. An external analyzer must resolve both sleep and active states at the declared electrical boundary. Its configured range, bandwidth, calibration, voltage measurement, and integration interval determine whether it can do so; a fast sample rate or product name alone is insufficient. RAPL and NVML do not supply this microcontroller measurement, and parameter count omits duty-cycle and peripheral costs.", "type": "single", "options": ["Sampling the Linux RAPL interface at 100 Hz to capture CPU package power states.", "Querying NVML/nvidia-smi programmatic bindings to integrate instantaneous milliwatt metrics.", "Multiplying model parameter count by a theoretical constant of 5 pJ per parameter.", "Using a calibrated external analyzer with verified current range, analog bandwidth, synchronized voltage measurement, and integration windows covering sleep, active execution, and transitions."], "answer": 3, "hints": ["Think about: select instrumentation capable of measuring the declared duty cycle and electrical boundary of a TinyML system"]}, {"q": "To determine the total environmental energy impact of an AI workload, an engineering team can simply sum the measured energy of the GPUs, CPUs, DRAM, and network interface cards reported by hardware telemetry.", "explanation": "False. Component measurements must cover disjoint IT loads over the same interval; GPU telemetry that includes device memory must not be added to that memory again. Restore omitted support loads, then use facility meters or a time-matched PUE to include non-IT overhead. Electricity alone still omits manufacturing and other lifecycle impacts.", "type": "truefalse", "answer": false, "hints": ["Think about: evaluate the requirement of incorporating facility overhead (PUE) into comprehensive AI energy accounting"]}]}, {"type": "reveal", "prompt": "Explain it: Explain how the Energy-Delay Product ($\\text{EDP} = E \\times t$) guides operating point selection in TinyML systems, and identify how voltage scaling interacts with clock frequency to minimize EDP.", "md": "EDP compares energy multiplied by execution time across feasible operating points. First exclude settings that miss quality or deadline requirements, then measure energy and delay for supported voltage-frequency combinations. Lower voltage can reduce switching energy, but a longer runtime adds static and support energy. The measured curves determine the optimum; the formula does not guarantee a moderate frequency or a universally beneficial voltage reduction."}, {"type": "summary", "points": ["**Operating energy**: The time integral of operating power across a declared set of devices and facility consumers."]}], "terms": [{"term": "Operating energy", "def": "The time integral of operating power across a declared set of devices and facility consumers."}], "id": "v2-15-04-energy-measurement-and-modeling", "chapter": "v2-15", "kind": "lesson", "minutes": 5}, "v2-15-04b-lab-energy": {"title": "Lab: where does the energy go?", "summary": "Split a workload's energy into data movement and arithmetic and see why on-chip reuse matters.", "minutes": 15, "blocks": [{"type": "text", "md": "Energy follows the same split as time: $E \\approx D_{vol} E_{move} + O\\,E_{op}$. The coefficients used in this lab are illustrative orders of magnitude: about 100 pJ per byte from DRAM, about 10 pJ per byte from on-chip SRAM, and about 1 pJ for a 16-bit multiply-add. The exact values depend on the chip; the ratio is what drives design."}, {"type": "widget", "id": "v2-15-lab-energy-widget", "widget": "energy", "title": "Try it: DRAM against on-chip reuse", "params": {"dvol": 1000000000000.0, "emove": 100, "ops": 10000000000000.0, "eop": 1}, "challenge": {"md": "The workload moves 1 TB and performs 10¹³ operations. Serve the bytes from **on-chip SRAM** (10 pJ/byte) instead of DRAM. What is the total energy in joules?", "answer": 20, "tolerance": 0.1, "unit": "J", "hint": "10¹² × 10 pJ plus 10¹³ × 1 pJ.", "explanation": "10 J for movement plus 10 J for compute = 20 J, down from 110 J with DRAM. Same arithmetic, 5.5x less energy."}}, {"type": "playground", "id": "v2-15-lab-energy-play", "title": "Energy model", "md": "Complete `energy_j` and `movement_share`.", "code": "def energy_j(bytes_moved, pj_per_byte, ops, pj_per_op):\n    # TODO: total joules\n    return None\n\n\ndef movement_share(bytes_moved, pj_per_byte, ops, pj_per_op):\n    # TODO: fraction of the energy spent moving data\n    return None\n\n\nprint(energy_j(1e12, 100, 1e13, 1), movement_share(1e12, 100, 1e13, 1))\n", "check": "assert abs(energy_j(1e12, 100, 1e13, 1) - 110) < 1e-9\nassert abs(energy_j(1e12, 10, 1e13, 1) - 20) < 1e-9\nassert abs(movement_share(1e12, 100, 1e13, 1) - 100 / 110) < 1e-9", "hint": "pJ are 10⁻¹² J: `(bytes_moved * pj_per_byte + ops * pj_per_op) * 1e-12`.", "solution": "def energy_j(bytes_moved, pj_per_byte, ops, pj_per_op):\n    return (bytes_moved * pj_per_byte + ops * pj_per_op) * 1e-12\n\n\ndef movement_share(bytes_moved, pj_per_byte, ops, pj_per_op):\n    move = bytes_moved * pj_per_byte\n    return move / (move + ops * pj_per_op)\n\n\nprint(energy_j(1e12, 100, 1e13, 1), 'J,', round(100 * movement_share(1e12, 100, 1e13, 1)), '% moving data')\n"}, {"type": "quiz", "id": "v2-15-lab-energy-quiz", "title": "Check your numbers", "questions": [{"type": "numeric", "q": "With DRAM at 100 pJ/byte, what percentage of the lab workload's energy is data movement (whole number)?", "answer": 91, "tolerance": 1, "unit": "%", "explanation": "100 / 110 = 91%: arithmetic is the small part.", "hints": ["100 J of 110 J."]}, {"type": "single", "q": "Which design change attacks the largest energy term here?", "options": ["Faster arithmetic units", "Keeping operands on chip and reusing them (tiling, systolic arrays)", "A higher clock", "More DRAM capacity"], "answer": 1, "explanation": "Reuse cuts the bytes fetched from DRAM; that is the motivation behind tiling, systolic arrays and operator fusion.", "hints": ["Look at the 91%."]}]}, {"type": "summary", "points": ["E ≈ bytes × energy per byte + ops × energy per op.", "An off-chip byte costs on the order of 100x a 16-bit multiply-add.", "On-chip reuse saves energy as well as time."]}], "source": {"chapter": "v2-15", "sections": ["hands-on lab"]}, "id": "v2-15-04b-lab-energy", "chapter": "v2-15", "kind": "lesson"}, "v2-15-05-carbon-footprint-calculation": {"title": "Carbon Footprint Calculation", "summary": "Compare the mathematical formulations for operational carbon and amortized embodied carbon.", "source": {"chapter": "v2-15", "sections": ["Carbon Footprint Calculation", "Carbon accountability and lifecycle phases", "Emission scopes and the development boundary", "Grid carbon intensity and regional variation", "Operational carbon calculation", "Embodied carbon assessment", "Lifecycle carbon accounting"]}, "blocks": [{"type": "text", "md": "By the end of this lesson you should be able to:\n\n- Compare the mathematical formulations for operational carbon and amortized embodied carbon\n- Analyze why grid decarbonization shifts the dominant lifecycle carbon component from operational to embodied emissions\n- Classify AI system emissions sources into Scope 1, Scope 2, and Scope 3 under the GHG Protocol\n- Explain how grid carbon intensity translates electrical energy into operational carbon emissions\n- Calculate the crossover point where cumulative inference energy exceeds one-time training energy"}, {"type": "from_book", "source": "Vol II, Sustainable AI, § Carbon Footprint Calculation", "md": "Consider a data center supplied by a hydro-heavy grid. Its operational carbon intensity can be far below a fossil-heavy grid, but hydroelectric generation is not lifecycle-zero, and the AI system is not carbon-free. Reservoir construction and operation have impacts, while mining the silicon, manufacturing the GPUs, and pouring the concrete for the data center emitted carbon before the servers were turned on. A complete footprint calculation must account for operational electricity, embodied carbon, and the ecological effects excluded from a carbon-only metric."}, {"type": "callout", "kind": "note", "title": "What the section covers", "md": "In the book this section runs through:\n\n- Carbon accountability and lifecycle phases\n- Emission scopes and the development boundary\n- Grid carbon intensity and regional variation\n- Operational carbon calculation\n- Embodied carbon assessment\n- Lifecycle carbon accounting\n\nPay attention to: operational vs embodied carbon equations; impact of grid decarbonization on embodied share; GHG Protocol Scope 1/2/3 classification; lifecycle carbon calculation steps."}, {"type": "reveal", "prompt": "Think first: When a hyperscaler relocates a training cluster from a fossil-heavy grid (800 $\\text{gCO}_2/\\text{kWh}$) to a clean hydro grid (20 $\\text{gCO}_2/\\text{kWh}$), its sustainability dashboard reveals that embodied carbon becomes the dominant component of total lifecycle emissions. Explain the mechanism behind this inversion and its implications for hardware procurement.", "md": "With facility energy held constant, changing the scenario grid intensity from 800 to 20 gCO2/kWh reduces operational emissions by 97.5%, while manufacturing emissions are unchanged. The embodied share therefore rises, but its final percentage requires the actual manufacturing footprint and lifetime energy. Procurement should compare lifetime extension and replacement using that lifecycle boundary rather than assume a universal embodied-carbon share."}, {"type": "quiz", "id": "v2-15-05-quiz", "title": "Check your understanding", "questions": [{"q": "Which equation pair correctly formalizes operational carbon emissions and daily amortized embodied carbon for AI hardware according to the chapter's accounting methodology?", "explanation": "The correct answer is D. $C_{\\text{operational}} = E_{\\text{total}} \\times \\text{CI}_{\\text{grid}}$ (where $E_{\\text{total}} = E_{\\text{IT}} \\times \\text{PUE}$) and $C_{\\text{embodied,daily}} = \\frac{C_{\\text{manufacturing}}}{T_{\\text{lifetime}} \\times 365}$. Operational carbon is facility energy (IT energy scaled by PUE) multiplied by grid carbon intensity, and embodied carbon is total manufacturing emissions divided by expected lifetime in days ($T_{\\text{lifetime}} \\times 365$). Dividing IT energy by PUE inverts facility overhead; adding energy to carbon intensity mixes incompatible units; and multiplying facility energy by PUE a second time double-counts facility overhead.", "type": "single", "options": ["$C_{\\text{operational}} = E_{\\text{IT}} / \\text{PUE}$ and $C_{\\text{embodied,daily}} = C_{\\text{manufacturing}} \\times T_{\\text{lifetime}}$.", "$C_{\\text{operational}} = E_{\\text{total}} + \\text{CI}_{\\text{grid}}$ and $C_{\\text{embodied,daily}} = C_{\\text{manufacturing}} / 365$.", "$C_{\\text{operational}} = E_{\\text{total}} \\times \\text{PUE} \\times \\text{CI}_{\\text{grid}}$ and $C_{\\text{embodied,daily}} = C_{\\text{manufacturing}} \\times 365 / T_{\\text{lifetime}}$.", "$C_{\\text{operational}} = E_{\\text{total}} \\times \\text{CI}_{\\text{grid}}$ (where $E_{\\text{total}} = E_{\\text{IT}} \\times \\text{PUE}$) and $C_{\\text{embodied,daily}} = \\frac{C_{\\text{manufacturing}}}{T_{\\text{lifetime}} \\times 365}$."], "answer": 3, "hints": ["Think about: compare the mathematical formulations for operational carbon and amortized embodied carbon"]}, {"q": "An engineering team is auditing the greenhouse gas emissions of an AI platform. Which option correctly maps the five operational sources to their respective GHG Protocol scopes?\n\n- S1: Diesel fuel burned by backup generators during a power outage at an owned facility.\n- S2: Grid electricity purchased to power an owned GPU cluster.\n- S3: Grid electricity consumed by cooling chillers in the same owned facility.\n- S4: Embodied carbon from manufacturing the GPUs at an external foundry.\n- S5: Electrical energy consumed by end-user smartphones running the deployed mobile inference app.", "explanation": "The correct answer is A. S1: Scope 1; S2: Scope 2; S3: Scope 2; S4: Scope 3; S5: Scope 3. Scope 1 encompasses direct emissions from owned combustion sources (on-site diesel generators). Scope 2 covers indirect emissions from purchased electricity (powering servers and facility cooling equipment). Scope 3 covers value-chain emissions upstream (semiconductor manufacturing at external foundries) and downstream (end-user mobile device execution). Classifying diesel as Scope 2 confuses direct combustion with purchased utility power; classifying cooling electricity as Scope 1 treats purchased utility power as on-site combustion; and classifying upstream semiconductor fabrication as Scope 1 misattributes supplier manufacturing to direct operations.", "type": "single", "options": ["S1: Scope 1; S2: Scope 2; S3: Scope 2; S4: Scope 3; S5: Scope 3.", "S1: Scope 2; S2: Scope 1; S3: Scope 2; S4: Scope 3; S5: Scope 3.", "S1: Scope 1; S2: Scope 2; S3: Scope 1; S4: Scope 2; S5: Scope 3.", "S1: Scope 3; S2: Scope 2; S3: Scope 2; S4: Scope 1; S5: Scope 1."], "answer": 0, "hints": ["Think about: classify AI system emissions sources into Scope 1, Scope 2, and Scope 3 under the GHG Protocol"]}, {"q": "The metric representing the mass of carbon dioxide emitted per unit of electricity generated, expressed in grams of $\\text{CO}_2$ per kilowatt-hour, is known as `____`.", "explanation": "carbon intensity. Grid carbon intensity ($\\text{gCO}_2/\\text{kWh}$) varies by location and time, governing the operational emissions associated with electricity consumption.", "type": "fill", "answer": ["carbon intensity"], "hints": ["Think about: explain how grid carbon intensity translates electrical energy into operational carbon emissions"]}, {"q": "A production language model consumed 1,200 MWh during its one-time training run. In production, it serves 8 million requests per day, consuming 0.0005 kWh per request. After how many days of continuous serving will the cumulative inference energy reach the one-time training energy?", "explanation": "The correct answer is B. 300 days. Daily inference energy is \\$8{,}000{,}000\\text{ requests/day} \\times 0.0005\\text{ kWh/request} = 4{,}000\\text{ kWh/day} = 4\\text{ MWh/day}$. Dividing total training energy by daily inference energy yields \\$1{,}200\\text{ MWh} / 4\\text{ MWh/day} = 300\\text{ days}$. A calculation yielding 120 days underestimates the crossover period; 600 days assumes half the daily inference volume; and 1,200 days erroneously assumes 1 MWh/day serving energy.", "type": "single", "options": ["120 days.", "300 days.", "600 days.", "1,200 days."], "answer": 1, "hints": ["Think about: calculate the crossover point where cumulative inference energy exceeds one-time training energy"]}, {"q": "Give one valid order for the following steps in computing the specified operational-plus-accelerator-manufacturing carbon subtotal of a model training workload:", "explanation": "One valid order is: (3) measure or estimate raw IT electrical power and duration across GPUs, CPUs, and memory, (1) multiply IT energy by facility PUE to obtain total facility energy consumption, (5) multiply total facility energy by regional grid carbon intensity to determine operational carbon, (2) amortize accelerator manufacturing footprint over total operational lifetime to calculate the job's embodied share, (4) sum operational and embodied carbon to determine the accounted subtotal. IT energy must be measured first; facility PUE is applied to calculate total electrical draw; grid carbon intensity converts facility energy into operational emissions; embodied manufacturing carbon is amortized over the run's duration; and both components are summed for this partial lifecycle account. Manufacturing of hosts, networking, storage, and facilities, and any other lifecycle stages outside the stated inventory, must be added before claiming a complete footprint. PUE and carbon intensity may be multiplied in either order, but facility overhead must be included exactly once; embodied carbon can be computed independently before the final sum.", "type": "order", "items": ["measure or estimate raw IT electrical power and duration across GPUs, CPUs, and memory", "multiply IT energy by facility PUE to obtain total facility energy consumption", "multiply total facility energy by regional grid carbon intensity to determine operational carbon.", "amortize accelerator manufacturing footprint over total operational lifetime to calculate the job's embodied share", "sum operational and embodied carbon to determine the accounted subtotal"], "hints": ["Think about: analyze the sequential calculation workflow for AI lifecycle carbon accounting"]}]}, {"type": "summary", "points": ["**Operational carbon**: Emissions attributed to operating energy under a stated facility boundary and grid-carbon signal.", "**Embodied carbon**: Emissions from manufacturing and other non-operating lifecycle processes allocated to the measured system or workload."]}], "terms": [{"term": "Operational carbon", "def": "Emissions attributed to operating energy under a stated facility boundary and grid-carbon signal."}, {"term": "Embodied carbon", "def": "Emissions from manufacturing and other non-operating lifecycle processes allocated to the measured system or workload."}], "id": "v2-15-05-carbon-footprint-calculation", "chapter": "v2-15", "kind": "lesson", "minutes": 5}, "v2-15-06-data-center-energy-and-resource": {"title": "Data Center Energy and Resource Consumption", "summary": "Explain how power delivery infrastructure and utility interconnect lead times constrain large-scale AI cluster deployments.", "source": {"chapter": "v2-15", "sections": ["Data Center Energy and Resource Consumption", "Data center energy and AI workloads", "Power delivery", "Cooling energy, heat rejection, and water"]}, "blocks": [{"type": "text", "md": "By the end of this lesson you should be able to:\n\n- Explain how power delivery infrastructure and utility interconnect lead times constrain large-scale AI cluster deployments\n- Assign power-delivery losses to the correct side of the PUE accounting boundary\n- Calculate facility power draw and carbon emissions using PUE and grid carbon intensity to evaluate environmental impact\n- Evaluate the water-energy trade-off between evaporative and dry heat rejection"}, {"type": "from_book", "source": "Vol II, Sustainable AI, § Data Center Energy and Resource Consumption", "md": "A training cluster can fit its accelerator power budget yet exceed its facility budget once support systems and cooling enter the account. Whether a facility can host the cluster at all is a feasibility question; the sustainability question is whether a change in power delivery or cooling reduces lifecycle cost while preserving that feasible operating envelope. The relevant comparison includes annual energy, local water use, equipment lifetime, and the energy wasted when cooling failures interrupt useful work."}, {"type": "callout", "kind": "note", "title": "What the section covers", "md": "In the book this section runs through:\n\n- Data center energy and AI workloads\n- Power delivery\n- Cooling energy, heat rejection, and water\n\nPay attention to: power delivery bottlenecks and substation lead times; which conversion losses fall inside the PUE boundary; PUE versus grid carbon intensity; heat rejection water versus fan energy."}, {"type": "reveal", "prompt": "Think first: A cloud provider can procure and install 10,000 GPUs within six months, but building the required electrical substation and securing utility interconnect approval takes 24 months. Explain why electrical power delivery represents the binding bottleneck for data center expansion, and state the systems consequence of this lead-time disparity.", "md": "In this scenario, the 24-month power-delivery path is four times the six-month GPU-procurement path and gates operation at the intended scale. Delivered accelerators can remain electrically stranded until the connection is ready. Actual lead times and alternatives are site-specific, so procurement must follow a qualified supply schedule rather than assume this ratio applies everywhere."}, {"type": "quiz", "id": "v2-15-06-quiz", "title": "Check your understanding", "questions": [{"q": "A facility audit lists conversion losses in five places: the utility transformer, the online UPS, the facility power distribution units, the server power supplies, and the baseboard voltage regulators. Which losses raise the facility's PUE?", "explanation": "The correct answer is A. PUE divides facility energy by IT energy, so only losses outside the IT boundary add to the overhead: the transformer, UPS, and facility distribution. Server power supplies and baseboard regulators are part of the measured IT load and appear in both numerator and denominator, although their heat still loads the cooling system. Counting every loss as overhead double counts the IT-side terms, and PUE covers all non-IT facility load, not only cooling.", "type": "single", "options": ["The transformer, UPS, and facility distribution losses, because they lie upstream of the IT boundary; server power supplies and voltage regulators sit inside the measured IT load.", "All five, because every conversion loss turns delivered power into heat that the cooling system must remove.", "Only the server power supplies and voltage regulators, because they sit closest to the accelerators that do the useful work.", "None of them, because PUE measures cooling overhead and conversion losses are electrical rather than thermal."], "answer": 0, "hints": ["Think about: assign power-delivery losses to the correct side of the PUE accounting boundary"]}]}, {"type": "reveal", "prompt": "Explain it: Facility A reports a Power Usage Effectiveness (PUE) of 1.08 on an electrical grid with carbon intensity of \\$450 g CO_2/kWh. Facility B reports PUE 1.35 on a grid with \\$50 g CO_2/kWh. For a \\$10 MW IT compute load, calculate the total hourly electrical power drawn and carbon emissions for both facilities, and explain the limitation of using PUE as a sole environmental metric.", "md": "At \\$10 MW IT load: Facility A draws \\$10 MW × 1.08 = 10.8 MW (\\$10,800 kW) total power, emitting \\$10,800 kW × 450 g CO_2/kWh = 4,860 kg CO_2/hr (\\$4.86 tonnes/hr). Facility B draws \\$10 MW × 1.35 = 13.5 MW (\\$13,500 kW) total power, emitting \\$13,500 kW × 50 g CO_2/kWh = 675 kg CO_2/hr (\\$0.675 tonnes/hr). Facility B emits approx 7.2× less carbon than Facility A despite having a significantly worse PUE (1.35 vs. 1.08). PUE measures only facility power relative to IT-equipment power, not the cleanliness of the electricity source, so optimizing PUE in isolation can mislead teams into high-carbon grid locations."}, {"type": "reveal", "prompt": "Explain it: A 10 MW facility in a water-stressed region can reject heat through evaporative cooling towers or through dry coolers. Explain the water and energy trade-off between the two, and why the operator might accept higher fan energy.", "md": "Evaporation carries large amounts of heat with a small water flow because each kilogram evaporated removes its latent heat, but the evaporated water is consumed and must be replaced, which shows up as WUE. Dry coolers avoid that direct water consumption, but their capacity depends on the coolant-to-ambient temperature difference, so hot weather reduces capacity or requires more fan power. In a water-stressed watershed the local water burden can outweigh the added electricity, so the operator may choose dry or hybrid rejection while still counting the upstream water of the electricity it buys."}, {"type": "summary", "points": ["You can now explain how power delivery infrastructure and utility interconnect lead times constrain large-scale AI cluster deployments.", "You can now assign power-delivery losses to the correct side of the PUE accounting boundary.", "You can now calculate facility power draw and carbon emissions using PUE and grid carbon intensity to evaluate environmental impact.", "You can now evaluate the water-energy trade-off between evaporative and dry heat rejection."]}], "id": "v2-15-06-data-center-energy-and-resource", "chapter": "v2-15", "kind": "lesson", "minutes": 5}, "v2-15-07-training-vs-inference-energy-ana": {"title": "Training vs. Inference Energy Analysis", "summary": "Analyze why cumulative inference energy dominates one-time training energy for large-scale production models.", "source": {"chapter": "v2-15", "sections": ["Training vs. Inference Energy Analysis", "Carbon benchmarks across AI workloads", "Training energy demands", "Inference energy costs", "Edge execution and the fleet energy account"]}, "blocks": [{"type": "text", "md": "By the end of this lesson you should be able to:\n\n- Analyze why cumulative inference energy dominates one-time training energy for large-scale production models\n- Analyze communication-energy mechanisms and estimate their contribution from phase power and duration\n- Explain the microarchitectural mechanism behind the decode phase's low energy efficiency in LLM serving\n- Evaluate an on-device update against battery, memory, thermal, and quality requirements and identify alternatives when it exceeds them\n- Calculate average power in duty-cycled TinyML systems and explain how duty cycling extends operational battery life"}, {"type": "from_book", "source": "Vol II, Sustainable AI, § Training vs. Inference Energy Analysis", "md": "Training a large language model is a concentrated, highly visible energy event that consumes thousands of megawatt-hours over weeks or months. Serving the same model to a billion daily queries is the opposite shape of load: energy spent continuously, query by query, year after year."}, {"type": "callout", "kind": "note", "title": "What the section covers", "md": "In the book this section runs through:\n\n- Carbon benchmarks across AI workloads\n- Training energy demands\n- Inference energy costs\n- Edge execution and the fleet energy account\n\nPay attention to: cumulative inference overtaking one-time training; communication energy in distributed training; decode memory-bandwidth bottleneck and static leakage; on-device learning and the battery wall."}, {"type": "reveal", "prompt": "Think first: A distributed training job expands from 1,024 to 2,048 accelerators. Explain which mechanisms can increase communication energy and what measurements establish its share of step energy for the actual run.", "md": "Topology, collective algorithm, message volume, overlap, stragglers, and powered waiting determine the energy change; a larger participant count does not guarantee super-linear growth for every collective. Estimate phase energy from measured power integrated over each phase and compare with the whole-step account. Exposed communication time equals its energy fraction only under an explicit equal-average-power approximation; continued power draw alone does not justify that equality."}, {"type": "quiz", "id": "v2-15-07-quiz", "title": "Check your understanding", "questions": [{"q": "Which systems analogy and operational reality best explains why inference often dominates total lifecycle energy for widely deployed foundation models?", "explanation": "The correct answer is B. A training run concentrates energy in a bounded interval, while serving repeats for the product's lifetime. Sufficient demand can make cumulative serving energy exceed training energy, but volume, workload mix, idle capacity, and updates determine the crossover. Neither instantaneous power nor an assumed backward pass establishes it; inference can use specialized accelerators.", "type": "single", "options": ["Inference requires higher instantaneous wattage per accelerator than training because real-time serving disables dynamic voltage scaling.", "Training is like a one-time rocket launch that burns concentrated energy over several weeks, whereas inference is like operating a commercial airline fleet that burns energy continuously across billions of recurring user interactions over years.", "Inference models must perform a full backward pass on every user query to maintain accuracy, doubling computational cost.", "Inference cannot run on specialized accelerators, forcing cloud operators to use energy-inefficient legacy CPUs."], "answer": 1, "hints": ["Think about: analyze why cumulative inference energy dominates one-time training energy for large-scale production models"]}, {"q": "Profiling an autoregressive LLM serving pipeline shows that the prefill phase achieves 55 percent Model FLOPs Utilization (MFU), while the token-generation decode phase achieves only 6 percent MFU with high HBM bandwidth utilization. What is the physical mechanism causing decode energy inefficiency?", "explanation": "The correct answer is D. High HBM utilization with low MFU is consistent with a bandwidth-bound decode phase whose limited batch reuse leaves arithmetic units underused. Powered waiting can add static and support energy. These measurements do not establish a fixed prefill-to-decode energy ratio; compare measured energy under declared batch, context, output quality, latency, and hardware boundaries. Decode does not require backpropagation.", "type": "single", "options": ["Decode executes in FP32 precision while prefill executes in INT4 precision.", "Prefill bypasses high-bandwidth memory entirely by storing all weights in CPU host RAM.", "Decode requires running backpropagation to generate key-value cache embeddings for each new token.", "Decode is memory-bandwidth bound: each generated token requires reading all model weights from HBM with low arithmetic intensity, leaving tensor execution units idling while drawing static leakage and clock distribution power."], "answer": 3, "hints": ["Think about: explain the microarchitectural mechanism behind the decode phase's low energy efficiency in LLM serving"]}, {"q": "Order the stages of a hierarchical wake-word cascade designed to minimize average energy consumption on a smart speaker from lowest power to highest power:", "explanation": "The correct order is: (3) ultra-low-power Voice Activity Detection (VAD) circuit listening continuously at microwatt power, (2) small convolutional or recurrent neural network wake-word classifier verifying the trigger phrase, (1) large language model or full automatic speech recognition running on host processor or cloud. The cascade minimizes energy by placing the cheapest filter first: the microwatt VAD filters silence/ambient noise; upon detecting voice, it triggers the small milliwatt wake-word model; only when the trigger phrase is validated does the system power up the watt-scale host processor or cloud network stack for full inference. Inverting the sequence would burn hundreds of milliwatts or watts on continuous ambient silence.", "type": "order", "items": ["small convolutional or recurrent neural network wake-word classifier verifying the trigger phrase", "large language model or full automatic speech recognition running on host processor or cloud"], "hints": ["Think about: design a hierarchical wake-word cascade to minimize average power consumption in low-power edge systems"]}]}, {"type": "reveal", "prompt": "Explain it: A mobile application team proposes running nightly on-device full backpropagation fine-tuning for a 1B-parameter model on user smartphones. Using the concept of the 'battery wall', explain how to assess a 5 percent overnight battery budget and identify alternatives if the measured workload exceeds it.", "md": "A 15 Wh battery contains 54,000 J, so a 5% budget permits 2,700 J. Whether a 1B-parameter training workload fits depends on its measured energy per token, training length, memory footprint, and thermal limits; parameter count alone cannot establish infeasibility. PEFT can reduce trainable parameters and optimizer state, but LoRA still backpropagates through the relevant network and need not remove activation storage. Profile the complete update and consider fewer local steps, off-device training, or charging-only execution."}, {"type": "reveal", "prompt": "Explain it: A TinyML sensor runs inference for 10 ms at 20 mW active power and sleeps for 990 ms at 20 $\\mu\\text{W}$ deep-sleep power during a 1-second monitoring period. Calculate the average power draw, and explain how duty cycling enables multi-year battery operation.", "md": "The active contribution to average power is \\$20 mW×0.010=0.20 mW; the sleep contribution is \\$0.020 mW×0.990=0.0198 mW. Their sum is \\$0.2198 mW, about 91 times below continuous active power. This saves energy, but multi-year operation depends on usable battery capacity, self-discharge, and peripheral loads. Even one year at this average requires about 1.93 Wh before those additional costs."}, {"type": "summary", "points": ["You can now analyze why cumulative inference energy dominates one-time training energy for large-scale production models.", "You can now analyze communication-energy mechanisms and estimate their contribution from phase power and duration.", "You can now explain the microarchitectural mechanism behind the decode phase's low energy efficiency in LLM serving.", "You can now evaluate an on-device update against battery, memory, thermal, and quality requirements and identify alternatives when it exceeds them."]}], "id": "v2-15-07-training-vs-inference-energy-ana", "chapter": "v2-15", "kind": "lesson", "minutes": 5}, "v2-15-08-hardware-lifecycle-and-e-waste": {"title": "Hardware Lifecycle and E-Waste", "summary": "Justify hardware lifespan extension as a high-leverage sustainability intervention using embodied carbon amortization.", "source": {"chapter": "v2-15", "sections": ["Hardware Lifecycle and E-Waste", "Design and experimentation phase", "Water, chemicals, and critical materials", "Manufacturing phase", "Use phase", "Disposal, e-waste, and embedded AI", "Hardware lifespan extension"]}, "blocks": [{"type": "text", "md": "By the end of this lesson you should be able to:\n\n- Justify hardware lifespan extension as a high-leverage sustainability intervention using embodied carbon amortization\n- Analyze why development-phase experimentation overhead must be included in AI sustainability accounting and identify mitigation practices\n- Evaluate how zero-carbon electricity sourcing changes emissions accounting while physical use-phase constraints remain binding\n- Explain the role of Life Cycle Assessment (LCA) methodology in quantifying end-to-end hardware environmental impact\n- Apply circular economy principles to hardware retirement and secondary redeployment decisions"}, {"type": "from_book", "source": "Vol II, Sustainable AI, § Hardware Lifecycle and E-Waste", "md": "The environmental cost of an AI accelerator begins long before its first FLOP is calculated. The per-H100 manufacturing footprint quantified in a later section is incurred before the device is ever powered on. A training fleet of a few thousand such accelerators therefore carries a manufacturing debt of the same order as the operational emissions of a GPT-3-scale training run on the US-average grid (about …)."}, {"type": "callout", "kind": "note", "title": "What the section covers", "md": "In the book this section runs through:\n\n- Design and experimentation phase\n- Water, chemicals, and critical materials\n- Manufacturing phase\n- Use phase\n- Disposal, e-waste, and embedded AI\n- Hardware lifespan extension\n\nPay attention to: accelerator lifespan extension and amortization; hidden carbon in experimentation and NAS; use-phase constraints on renewable grids; Life Cycle Assessment (LCA) methodology."}, {"type": "reveal", "prompt": "Think first: A research paper reports that training a new vision-language model consumed 500 MWh for its final successful training run. Explain why reporting only the final run systematically underestimates the development phase's total carbon footprint, and identify two mitigation practices.", "md": "The final run omits exploratory searches, debugging, failed runs, compilation, and evaluation. Their cost depends on the actual experiment inventory; fifty candidates at one-tenth of final-run cost add five final-run equivalents in the chapter's example. Transfer learning, shared search, and early stopping can reduce development work, provided the complete comparison preserves the required quality."}, {"type": "quiz", "id": "v2-15-08-quiz", "title": "Check your understanding", "questions": [{"q": "For a fixed inventoried manufacturing footprint, what happens to annualized embodied carbon when useful hardware life extends from three years to five years, before considering changes in operational energy?", "explanation": "The correct answer is A. Dividing the same manufacturing footprint by five years rather than three changes its annual allocation by a factor of 3/5, a 40% reduction. This does not erase manufacturing emissions or prove that retaining hardware minimizes total lifecycle carbon. The comparison also needs useful work, operational energy, replacement manufacturing, and consistent component boundaries. Aging does not automatically improve silicon efficiency or facility PUE.", "type": "single", "options": ["The fixed manufacturing footprint is amortized over five years rather than three, reducing its annualized allocation by 40 percent; total lifecycle savings still depend on operational energy and the inventory boundary.", "Older accelerators automatically consume 40 percent less electrical power per FLOP as silicon pathways break in over time.", "Extending accelerator lifespans from three to five years eliminates electronic waste entirely because hardware decomposes organically after five years.", "Data center PUE automatically drops to 1.0 after servers have operated for more than four years."], "answer": 0, "hints": ["Think about: justify hardware lifespan extension as a high-leverage sustainability intervention using embodied carbon amortization"]}, {"q": "If an AI training data center is powered 100 percent by dedicated geothermal and hydroelectric power, the use phase is completely unconstrained and engineers no longer need to consider grid dynamics or cooling efficiency.", "explanation": "False. Low-carbon sourcing does not remove electrical capacity, thermal, water, or reliability limits, and it does not erase generation or hardware lifecycle burdens. Any effect on other grid generation depends on the actual supply and grid connection; dedicated hydro or geothermal alone does not establish a peaker response.", "type": "truefalse", "answer": false, "hints": ["Think about: evaluate how zero-carbon electricity sourcing changes emissions accounting while physical use-phase constraints remain binding"]}, {"q": "Standardized under ISO 14040 and ISO 14044, a comprehensive `____` evaluates the cumulative environmental impact of AI hardware across raw material extraction, manufacturing, use, and end-of-life disposal.", "explanation": "life cycle assessment. A Life Cycle Assessment (LCA) quantifies environmental impacts across design, supply-chain fabrication, active operational use, and recycling/disposal, ensuring embodied emissions are not ignored.", "type": "fill", "answer": ["life cycle assessment"], "hints": ["Think about: explain the role of Life Cycle Assessment (LCA) methodology in quantifying end-to-end hardware environmental impact"]}, {"q": "A cloud provider considers retiring functional accelerators because a new generation offers a 12 percent improvement in performance per watt. Which option identifies a circular-economy candidate for evaluation, without claiming that this efficiency specification alone determines the lowest-emissions choice?", "explanation": "The correct answer is C. Compatible secondary use can avoid premature disposal and new procurement, but it must meet the workload's quality, performance, security, and reliability requirements. Compare useful-work energy, grid intensity, utilization, replacement manufacturing, and remaining life. A 12 percent performance-per-watt specification alone cannot establish the emissions minimum or its break-even time; automatic replacement ignores the new embodied burden.", "type": "single", "options": ["Immediately shred and dispose of all prior-generation accelerators to eliminate any hardware with lower peak energy efficiency.", "Replace all servers instantly because any improvement in operational efficiency automatically offsets the embodied carbon of manufacturing a replacement fleet.", "Evaluate redeployment to compatible secondary workloads, comparing continued useful service with replacement manufacturing and measured operating emissions over the same remaining service interval.", "Overclock the older accelerators beyond thermal limits to match new hardware throughput regardless of cooling failure risk."], "answer": 2, "hints": ["Think about: apply circular economy principles to hardware retirement and secondary redeployment decisions"]}]}, {"type": "summary", "points": ["You can now justify hardware lifespan extension as a high-leverage sustainability intervention using embodied carbon amortization.", "You can now analyze why development-phase experimentation overhead must be included in AI sustainability accounting and identify mitigation practices.", "You can now evaluate how zero-carbon electricity sourcing changes emissions accounting while physical use-phase constraints remain binding.", "You can now explain the role of Life Cycle Assessment (LCA) methodology in quantifying end-to-end hardware environmental impact."]}], "id": "v2-15-08-hardware-lifecycle-and-e-waste", "chapter": "v2-15", "kind": "lesson", "minutes": 5}, "v2-15-09-mitigation-strategies": {"title": "Mitigation Strategies", "summary": "Explain Jevons paradox in AI systems and justify why engineering efficiency must be paired with usage governance.", "source": {"chapter": "v2-15", "sections": ["Mitigation Strategies", "Multi-layer mitigation", "Lifecycle-aware development", "Infrastructure optimization", "Carbon-aware placement and scheduling", "Google sustainability framework case study"]}, "blocks": [{"type": "text", "md": "By the end of this lesson you should be able to:\n\n- Explain Jevons paradox in AI systems and justify why engineering efficiency must be paired with usage governance\n- Compare theoretical model compression techniques against hardware-realizable energy reductions on modern AI accelerators\n- Design architectural strategies that address data-movement dominance to mitigate the AI energy wall\n- Evaluate carbon-aware placement as a conditional mitigation candidate for feasible batch workloads\n- Distinguish attributional and consequential grid evidence and select evidence appropriate to a workload-shifting decision"}, {"type": "from_book", "source": "Vol II, Sustainable AI, § Mitigation Strategies", "md": "When a data center hits its absolute power ceiling, the operator cannot simply buy more GPUs. Supporting additional useful work requires reducing the power needed for that work, changing its schedule, or securing additional feasible capacity. Algorithmic optimization can reduce executed work and data movement; infrastructure changes can reduce overhead. Carbon-aware placement addresses a different constraint by changing the emissions associated with electricity use, even when energy demand stays fixed."}, {"type": "callout", "kind": "note", "title": "What the section covers", "md": "In the book this section runs through:\n\n- Multi-layer mitigation\n- Lifecycle-aware development\n- Infrastructure optimization\n- Carbon-aware placement and scheduling\n- Google sustainability framework case study\n\nPay attention to: Jevons paradox and demand rebound; hardware-realizable vs theoretical compression savings; event-driven and sparse architectures that avoid unneeded work; carbon-aware spatial and temporal scheduling."}, {"type": "reveal", "prompt": "Think first: A model compression team evaluates unstructured weight pruning, INT8 quantization, and knowledge distillation for serving a transformer. Explain the hardware mechanisms that can turn each technique into measured energy savings, and why theoretical compression alone is insufficient.", "md": "INT8 can reduce weight-transfer bytes relative to FP16 and use supported integer kernels; distillation can reduce dense operations and stored state at the required quality. Unstructured pruning saves energy only when the implementation exploits sparsity instead of moving and processing zeros or spending the savings on indexing. Compare joules per completed task at matched quality, precision assumptions, utilization, and measurement boundaries; no universal cross-precision energy multiplier follows from the operation count alone."}, {"type": "quiz", "id": "v2-15-09-quiz", "title": "Check your understanding", "questions": [{"q": "An AI company deploys quantization and knowledge distillation, reducing the energy cost per user query by 50 percent. Over the following year, the company's total data center electricity consumption increases by 75 percent as lower per-query costs enable new product features and attract 3.5$\\times$ more queries. Which phenomenon explains this outcome?", "explanation": "The correct answer is C. Jevons paradox, where technological efficiency improvements lower the effective cost of a resource, stimulating increased demand that outweighs per-unit efficiency gains. Jevons paradox (or the rebound effect) describes when per-unit efficiency gains make a service cheaper or faster, unlocking new use cases and user demand whose aggregate resource consumption exceeds the initial per-unit savings. This demonstrates why engineering efficiency alone cannot guarantee environmental sustainability without capacity governance or carbon budgets. Amdahl's law governs parallel compute speedup; Dennard scaling describes CMOS power physics; and the memory wall addresses processor-memory bandwidth disparities.", "type": "single", "options": ["Amdahl's law, which states that sequential execution limits parallel speedup.", "Dennard scaling breakdown, where transistor power density increases with scaling.", "Jevons paradox, where technological efficiency improvements lower the effective cost of a resource, stimulating increased demand that outweighs per-unit efficiency gains.", "The memory wall, where DRAM bandwidth fails to keep pace with arithmetic throughput."], "answer": 2, "hints": ["Think about: explain Jevons paradox in AI systems and justify why engineering efficiency must be paired with usage governance"]}, {"q": "A systems team aims to overcome the energy wall for future foundation model architectures. Which design direction most directly targets the physical root cause of energy waste identified in the chapter?", "explanation": "The correct answer is C. Event-driven and sparse-activation designs can avoid unnecessary computation and transfers when inputs or activations permit it. The saving depends on event rate, sensing and static power, sparse execution overhead, and achieved quality. Raising voltage increases dynamic power at fixed activity and frequency; keeping units active does not establish useful work per joule. Dense computation is not universally movement dominated.", "type": "single", "options": ["Increasing clock frequency and supply voltage to maximize compute throughput per unit silicon area.", "Enforcing dense, synchronized execution across all model layers so accelerator execution units maintain 100 percent active duty cycles.", "Adopting event-driven and sparse-activation architectures that execute compute only on salient tokens or state changes, minimizing dense data movement across memory hierarchies.", "Replacing FP16 tensor cores with FP32 standard execution units to maximize numeric accuracy per memory access."], "answer": 2, "hints": ["Think about: design architectural strategies that address data-movement dominance to mitigate the AI energy wall"]}, {"q": "A platform team has recurring batch training jobs that can move across regions or time while preserving quality. Which intervention is a candidate for lowering operational carbon without changing the model, provided capacity, data access, deadlines, and net emissions are checked?", "explanation": "The correct answer is D. Carbon-aware placement and scheduling can exploit lower-emissions windows for flexible jobs. Compare complete facility energy and compatible grid signals, including checkpoint, transfer, restart, and idle costs, after filtering infeasible candidates. Regional averages motivate investigation but do not guarantee an order-of-magnitude net saving. Changing batch size or activations changes the workload and may change its quality or convergence.", "type": "single", "options": ["Replacing all ReLU activation functions with GELU activations across training scripts.", "Increasing the batch size to maximize GPU execution unit occupancy.", "Switching from PyTorch to a C++ inference engine for training loops.", "Implementing carbon-aware spatial and temporal scheduling to route training jobs to data center regions and time windows with surplus renewable energy and low carbon intensity."], "answer": 3, "hints": ["Think about: evaluate carbon-aware placement as a conditional mitigation candidate for feasible batch workloads"]}, {"q": "Order the following stages of a carbon-aware scheduler's decision:", "explanation": "The correct order is: (2) obtain grid forecasts and workload requirements, (5) filter candidates by operational constraints, (1) compare complete projected emissions, (3) dispatch the selected feasible action, (4) monitor progress and re-evaluate. A lower-intensity region can be infeasible or lose its advantage once movement and waiting costs enter. Feasibility and net benefit must therefore precede dispatch, while progress monitoring can trigger another decision cycle.", "type": "order", "items": ["obtain documented grid forecasts and workload requirements", "filter candidates by deadline, capacity, data access, power, cooling, and reliability requirements.", "compare complete projected emissions of feasible candidates, including checkpoint, transfer, restart, and idle costs", "dispatch the selected feasible action", "monitor progress and re-evaluate against constraints"], "hints": ["Think about: analyze the operational execution pipeline of a carbon-aware workload scheduler managing distributed AI jobs"]}]}, {"type": "reveal", "prompt": "Explain it: Explain the difference between average and marginal grid emissions, and identify the evidence a scheduler needs to estimate the emissions effect of a proposed workload shift.", "md": "An average factor attributes an electricity mix over a specified place and period; it can be annual or time-resolved. A marginal factor estimates the emissions response to changing demand. A scheduler evaluating a proposed shift needs consequential evidence matched to its region, forecast horizon, and workload, with uncertainty recorded. A marginal estimate need not identify one directly observable generator, and the full comparison must also include shifting costs."}, {"type": "summary", "points": ["**Carbon-aware scheduling**: Choosing workload time or location using a declared carbon signal while respecting deadlines, capacity, and movement costs."]}], "terms": [{"term": "Carbon-aware scheduling", "def": "Choosing workload time or location using a declared carbon signal while respecting deadlines, capacity, and movement costs."}], "id": "v2-15-09-mitigation-strategies", "chapter": "v2-15", "kind": "lesson", "minutes": 5}, "v2-15-10-policy-regulation-and-the-path-f": {"title": "Policy, Regulation, and the Path Forward", "summary": "Explain why market incentives alone fail to drive carbon reduction and justify the role of carbon pricing policy.", "source": {"chapter": "v2-15", "sections": ["Policy, Regulation, and the Path Forward", "Regulatory mechanisms", "Industry self-regulation and standards", "Public engagement and transparency", "Environmental justice and responsible development", "Future research directions"]}, "blocks": [{"type": "text", "md": "By the end of this lesson you should be able to:\n\n- Explain why market incentives alone fail to drive carbon reduction and justify the role of carbon pricing policy\n- Analyze how sustainability regulations convert environmental accounting into mandatory software and infrastructure engineering requirements\n- Analyze how carbon taxation aligns financial cost optimization with carbon-aware infrastructure scheduling\n- Evaluate the difference between annual renewable energy netting (RECs) and 24/7 hourly clean energy matching\n- Explain how alternative architectures target movement energy and bound their benefit within the complete system account"}, {"type": "from_book", "source": "Vol II, Sustainable AI, § Policy, Regulation, and the Path Forward", "md": "Electricity prices reward some efficiency gains, but they need not capture water stress, manufacturing emissions, or local environmental burdens. A training cluster can reduce its electricity bill by moving to a cheaper region while increasing its emissions. If the bill omits that environmental cost, a cost-minimizing placement decision may favor the higher-emissions site. Reporting requirements expose the trade-off; carbon pricing, procurement conditions, and other policy instruments can change the incentives. Their effect depends on which activities they cover and how organizations implement them."}, {"type": "callout", "kind": "note", "title": "What the section covers", "md": "In the book this section runs through:\n\n- Regulatory mechanisms\n- Industry self-regulation and standards\n- Public engagement and transparency\n- Environmental justice and responsible development\n- Future research directions\n\nPay attention to: market failures and carbon pricing incentives; EU AI Act and CSRD regulatory compliance; 24/7 carbon-free energy (CFE) hourly matching vs annual RECs; non-von-Neumann computing paradigms."}, {"type": "reveal", "prompt": "Think first: Explain how an applicable emissions trading system or carbon tax changes a workload scheduler's cost objective, and what conditions determine whether cleaner placement becomes economically preferable.", "md": "A carbon price adds an emissions term to the cost objective. At equal facility energy and electricity tariffs, a higher applicable emissions factor produces a larger carbon charge. Cleaner placement becomes preferable only when the complete priced benefit exceeds transfer, waiting, runtime, and other costs and the candidate meets deadline, capacity, data, and reliability constraints. Carbon pricing changes incentives; it does not guarantee migration."}, {"type": "quiz", "id": "v2-15-10-quiz", "title": "Check your understanding", "questions": [{"q": "An industry observer claims that policy interventions and carbon pricing are unnecessary because 'free-market competition will naturally incentivize data center operators to minimize energy use and carbon emissions.' Which counterargument best explains why market mechanisms alone fail to guarantee sustainable AI deployment?", "explanation": "The correct answer is B. A financial objective can omit environmental externalities, so a cheaper feasible site need not have lower emissions or water stress. Policy can make those burdens visible or priced; carbon pricing is one mechanism rather than a prerequisite for every physical reduction. Electricity source alone does not determine silicon latency.", "type": "single", "options": ["Data center operators are legally restricted from purchasing power from renewable energy utilities.", "Electricity prices can omit emissions and other environmental burdens, so minimizing the electricity bill need not minimize the complete environmental account; pricing, reporting, or procurement requirements can change the decision incentives.", "AI models running on renewable electricity automatically suffer from higher inference latency than models on fossil power.", "Hardware vendors have already achieved the physical thermodynamic limit of computing efficiency, leaving no room for further optimization."], "answer": 1, "hints": ["Think about: explain why market incentives alone fail to drive carbon reduction and justify the role of carbon pricing policy"]}, {"q": "How do modern regulatory frameworks such as the Corporate Sustainability Reporting Directive (CSRD) and the EU AI Act directly affect the systems engineering practices of organizations deploying large-scale AI?", "explanation": "The correct answer is B. Reporting and AI governance frameworks can turn energy and sustainability evidence into a deployment obligation for covered entities or systems. Engineering teams need reproducible measurements, documented boundaries, and retained records matched to the applicable requirement. The frameworks have different scopes; they do not impose one universal Scope 1–3 telemetry rule on every AI deployment, nor the parameter bans or edge-only mandates in the other choices.", "type": "single", "options": ["They impose an absolute global cap banning the training of any machine learning model exceeding 100 billion parameters.", "For organizations and systems within their applicable scope, they create documentation and disclosure duties that require traceable energy and sustainability evidence; the exact obligations depend on the framework and deployment.", "They mandate that all AI inference must be executed exclusively on battery-powered mobile devices rather than cloud servers.", "They require that all machine learning models be retrained weekly using uncompressed full-precision floating-point arithmetic."], "answer": 1, "hints": ["Think about: analyze how sustainability regulations convert environmental accounting into mandatory software and infrastructure engineering requirements"]}, {"q": "If a technology enterprise purchases enough annual Renewable Energy Certificates (RECs) to match 100 percent of its data centers' annual electricity consumption, its evening foundation-model inference workloads running on a fossil-fueled regional grid can be accurately characterized as zero-carbon operations.", "explanation": "False. Annual certificates match electricity attributes over a year; they do not establish the emissions of an evening workload. Hourly matching makes unmatched intervals visible, but physical benefit still depends on deliverability, additional clean supply, storage, and grid conditions. Neither method eliminates hardware manufacturing emissions or proves a complete zero-carbon lifecycle.", "type": "truefalse", "answer": false, "hints": ["Think about: evaluate the difference between annual renewable energy netting (RECs) and 24/7 hourly clean energy matching"]}]}, {"type": "reveal", "prompt": "Explain it: Explain how future non-von-Neumann computing paradigms (such as neuromorphic computing and in-memory analog computing) address the fundamental physical bottleneck responsible for AI energy consumption.", "md": "These architectures can reduce selected transfers or unnecessary switching, but the gain depends on the workload and implementation. If movement contributes a fraction q of total energy, eliminating that term alone yields at most a 1/(1-q) reduction factor with other terms unchanged. Conversion, control, sensing, peripheral energy, training, and achieved quality remain in the comparison; neither a universal movement share nor near-zero system energy follows from the architecture label."}, {"type": "summary", "points": ["You can now explain why market incentives alone fail to drive carbon reduction and justify the role of carbon pricing policy.", "You can now analyze how sustainability regulations convert environmental accounting into mandatory software and infrastructure engineering requirements.", "You can now analyze how carbon taxation aligns financial cost optimization with carbon-aware infrastructure scheduling.", "You can now evaluate the difference between annual renewable energy netting (RECs) and 24/7 hourly clean energy matching."]}], "id": "v2-15-10-policy-regulation-and-the-path-f", "chapter": "v2-15", "kind": "lesson", "minutes": 5}, "v2-15-11-fallacies-and-pitfalls": {"title": "Fallacies and Pitfalls", "summary": "Evaluate the fallacy that cloud migration is inherently greener regardless of regional grid carbon intensity.", "source": {"chapter": "v2-15", "sections": ["Fallacies and Pitfalls", "Summary", "Summary"]}, "blocks": [{"type": "text", "md": "By the end of this lesson you should be able to:\n\n- Evaluate the fallacy that cloud migration is inherently greener regardless of regional grid carbon intensity\n- Analyze the pitfalls of localized optimization and apply full Life Cycle Assessment to evaluate net environmental impact\n- Evaluate carbon offsets against direct engineering interventions and justify prioritizing physical emissions reductions"}, {"type": "from_book", "source": "Vol II, Sustainable AI, § Fallacies and Pitfalls", "md": "Sustainability involves counterintuitive physics where efficiency improvements can increase total consumption and geographic choices can outweigh component-level optimizations. These fallacies and pitfalls capture errors that waste compute budgets and planetary resources through misallocated optimization effort."}, {"type": "callout", "kind": "note", "title": "What the section covers", "md": "Pay attention to: cloud-always-greener misconception; local optimization vs lifecycle trade-offs; carbon offsets vs direct emissions reductions."}, {"type": "reveal", "prompt": "Think first: Explain why an offset purchase does not replace evidence of physical emissions reductions, and what must be established before claiming a reduction from placement, efficiency, or hardware reuse.", "md": "An offset compensates through a separately credited activity; it does not change the workload's own electricity or manufacturing emissions. Additionality, durability, verification, and double counting affect that claim. Physical interventions need their own complete comparison: placement includes movement and waiting costs, efficiency preserves useful service, and reuse weighs avoided new manufacturing against continued operating energy. Report reductions separately from compensation and amortized allocation."}, {"type": "quiz", "id": "v2-15-11-quiz", "title": "Check your understanding", "questions": [{"q": "Migrating a machine learning training workload from an on-premises enterprise cluster in a low-carbon region (20 $\\text{gCO}_2/\\text{kWh}$) to a commercial cloud data center in a coal-heavy region (750 $\\text{gCO}_2/\\text{kWh}$) is guaranteed to reduce the workload's carbon footprint because hyperscale cloud providers operate at lower PUE.", "explanation": "False. Lower PUE alone does not guarantee lower emissions. At equal IT energy and the supplied grid factors, a PUE improvement must offset a 37.5-fold intensity increase merely to match electricity-related emissions. A complete comparison also needs actual energy, useful work, movement costs, and embodied allocations; cloud status is not evidence of that result.", "type": "truefalse", "answer": false, "hints": ["Think about: evaluate the fallacy that cloud migration is inherently greener regardless of regional grid carbon intensity"]}, {"q": "A systems team aggressively compresses a model using unstructured pruning to cut training FLOPs by 60 percent. In production, serving the sparse model requires specialized, custom-fabricated NPU accelerators and requires double the serving iterations to reach quality parity. Which pitfall does this scenario illustrate, and what is the proper engineering mitigation?", "explanation": "The correct answer is A. Local optimization of training compute without full-lifecycle assessment; the mitigation is conducting end-to-end Life Cycle Assessment (LCA) that accounts for hardware manufacturing, deployment scale, and serving energy before committing to architectural changes. Optimizing one isolated phase (training FLOPs) often shifts environmental burdens to other lifecycle phases, such as requiring new specialized hardware fabrication (embodied carbon) or increasing recurring inference queries. Comprehensive LCA across training, serving, and hardware manufacturing prevents counterproductive local optimizations. Amdahl's law does not address lifecycle trade-offs; reclassifying emissions scopes does not alter physical carbon; and workstation cooling is irrelevant to datacenter-scale deployment.", "type": "single", "options": ["Local optimization of training compute without full-lifecycle assessment; the mitigation is conducting end-to-end Life Cycle Assessment (LCA) that accounts for hardware manufacturing, deployment scale, and serving energy before committing to architectural changes.", "Failure to apply Amdahl's law; the mitigation is increasing CPU clock frequency.", "Violating the GHG Protocol; the mitigation is reclassifying Scope 3 emissions as Scope 1.", "Ignoring PUE; the mitigation is installing liquid cooling on the development workstations."], "answer": 0, "hints": ["Think about: analyze the pitfalls of localized optimization and apply full Life Cycle Assessment to evaluate net environmental impact"]}]}, {"type": "summary", "points": ["You can now evaluate the fallacy that cloud migration is inherently greener regardless of regional grid carbon intensity.", "You can now analyze the pitfalls of localized optimization and apply full Life Cycle Assessment to evaluate net environmental impact.", "You can now evaluate carbon offsets against direct engineering interventions and justify prioritizing physical emissions reductions."]}], "id": "v2-15-11-fallacies-and-pitfalls", "chapter": "v2-15", "kind": "lesson", "minutes": 5}, "v2-15-99-test": {"title": "Test: Sustainable AI", "kind": "test", "summary": "Check what you learned across the whole chapter.", "blocks": [{"type": "quiz", "id": "v2-15-test", "pass_score": 0.7, "questions": [{"q": "Order the stages of a hierarchical wake-word cascade designed to minimize average energy consumption on a smart speaker from lowest power to highest power:", "explanation": "The correct order is: (3) ultra-low-power Voice Activity Detection (VAD) circuit listening continuously at microwatt power, (2) small convolutional or recurrent neural network wake-word classifier verifying the trigger phrase, (1) large language model or full automatic speech recognition running on host processor or cloud. The cascade minimizes energy by placing the cheapest filter first: the microwatt VAD filters silence/ambient noise; upon detecting voice, it triggers the small milliwatt wake-word model; only when the trigger phrase is validated does the system power up the watt-scale host processor or cloud network stack for full inference. Inverting the sequence would burn hundreds of milliwatts or watts on continuous ambient silence.", "type": "order", "items": ["small convolutional or recurrent neural network wake-word classifier verifying the trigger phrase", "large language model or full automatic speech recognition running on host processor or cloud"], "hints": ["Think about: design a hierarchical wake-word cascade to minimize average power consumption in low-power edge systems"]}, {"q": "A cloud provider considers retiring functional accelerators because a new generation offers a 12 percent improvement in performance per watt. Which option identifies a circular-economy candidate for evaluation, without claiming that this efficiency specification alone determines the lowest-emissions choice?", "explanation": "The correct answer is C. Compatible secondary use can avoid premature disposal and new procurement, but it must meet the workload's quality, performance, security, and reliability requirements. Compare useful-work energy, grid intensity, utilization, replacement manufacturing, and remaining life. A 12 percent performance-per-watt specification alone cannot establish the emissions minimum or its break-even time; automatic replacement ignores the new embodied burden.", "type": "single", "options": ["Immediately shred and dispose of all prior-generation accelerators to eliminate any hardware with lower peak energy efficiency.", "Replace all servers instantly because any improvement in operational efficiency automatically offsets the embodied carbon of manufacturing a replacement fleet.", "Evaluate redeployment to compatible secondary workloads, comparing continued useful service with replacement manufacturing and measured operating emissions over the same remaining service interval.", "Overclock the older accelerators beyond thermal limits to match new hardware throughput regardless of cooling failure risk."], "answer": 2, "hints": ["Think about: apply circular economy principles to hardware retirement and secondary redeployment decisions"]}, {"q": "Standardized under ISO 14040 and ISO 14044, a comprehensive `____` evaluates the cumulative environmental impact of AI hardware across raw material extraction, manufacturing, use, and end-of-life disposal.", "explanation": "life cycle assessment. A Life Cycle Assessment (LCA) quantifies environmental impacts across design, supply-chain fabrication, active operational use, and recycling/disposal, ensuring embodied emissions are not ignored.", "type": "fill", "answer": ["life cycle assessment"], "hints": ["Think about: explain the role of Life Cycle Assessment (LCA) methodology in quantifying end-to-end hardware environmental impact"]}, {"q": "For a fixed inventoried manufacturing footprint, what happens to annualized embodied carbon when useful hardware life extends from three years to five years, before considering changes in operational energy?", "explanation": "The correct answer is A. Dividing the same manufacturing footprint by five years rather than three changes its annual allocation by a factor of 3/5, a 40% reduction. This does not erase manufacturing emissions or prove that retaining hardware minimizes total lifecycle carbon. The comparison also needs useful work, operational energy, replacement manufacturing, and consistent component boundaries. Aging does not automatically improve silicon efficiency or facility PUE.", "type": "single", "options": ["The fixed manufacturing footprint is amortized over five years rather than three, reducing its annualized allocation by 40 percent; total lifecycle savings still depend on operational energy and the inventory boundary.", "Older accelerators automatically consume 40 percent less electrical power per FLOP as silicon pathways break in over time.", "Extending accelerator lifespans from three to five years eliminates electronic waste entirely because hardware decomposes organically after five years.", "Data center PUE automatically drops to 1.0 after servers have operated for more than four years."], "answer": 0, "hints": ["Think about: justify hardware lifespan extension as a high-leverage sustainability intervention using embodied carbon amortization"]}, {"q": "A platform team has recurring batch training jobs that can move across regions or time while preserving quality. Which intervention is a candidate for lowering operational carbon without changing the model, provided capacity, data access, deadlines, and net emissions are checked?", "explanation": "The correct answer is D. Carbon-aware placement and scheduling can exploit lower-emissions windows for flexible jobs. Compare complete facility energy and compatible grid signals, including checkpoint, transfer, restart, and idle costs, after filtering infeasible candidates. Regional averages motivate investigation but do not guarantee an order-of-magnitude net saving. Changing batch size or activations changes the workload and may change its quality or convergence.", "type": "single", "options": ["Replacing all ReLU activation functions with GELU activations across training scripts.", "Increasing the batch size to maximize GPU execution unit occupancy.", "Switching from PyTorch to a C++ inference engine for training loops.", "Implementing carbon-aware spatial and temporal scheduling to route training jobs to data center regions and time windows with surplus renewable energy and low carbon intensity."], "answer": 3, "hints": ["Think about: evaluate carbon-aware placement as a conditional mitigation candidate for feasible batch workloads"]}, {"q": "Migrating a machine learning training workload from an on-premises enterprise cluster in a low-carbon region (20 $\\text{gCO}_2/\\text{kWh}$) to a commercial cloud data center in a coal-heavy region (750 $\\text{gCO}_2/\\text{kWh}$) is guaranteed to reduce the workload's carbon footprint because hyperscale cloud providers operate at lower PUE.", "explanation": "False. Lower PUE alone does not guarantee lower emissions. At equal IT energy and the supplied grid factors, a PUE improvement must offset a 37.5-fold intensity increase merely to match electricity-related emissions. A complete comparison also needs actual energy, useful work, movement costs, and embodied allocations; cloud status is not evidence of that result.", "type": "truefalse", "answer": false, "hints": ["Think about: evaluate the fallacy that cloud migration is inherently greener regardless of regional grid carbon intensity"]}, {"q": "An engineering team is auditing the greenhouse gas emissions of an AI platform. Which option correctly maps the five operational sources to their respective GHG Protocol scopes?\n\n- S1: Diesel fuel burned by backup generators during a power outage at an owned facility.\n- S2: Grid electricity purchased to power an owned GPU cluster.\n- S3: Grid electricity consumed by cooling chillers in the same owned facility.\n- S4: Embodied carbon from manufacturing the GPUs at an external foundry.\n- S5: Electrical energy consumed by end-user smartphones running the deployed mobile inference app.", "explanation": "The correct answer is A. S1: Scope 1; S2: Scope 2; S3: Scope 2; S4: Scope 3; S5: Scope 3. Scope 1 encompasses direct emissions from owned combustion sources (on-site diesel generators). Scope 2 covers indirect emissions from purchased electricity (powering servers and facility cooling equipment). Scope 3 covers value-chain emissions upstream (semiconductor manufacturing at external foundries) and downstream (end-user mobile device execution). Classifying diesel as Scope 2 confuses direct combustion with purchased utility power; classifying cooling electricity as Scope 1 treats purchased utility power as on-site combustion; and classifying upstream semiconductor fabrication as Scope 1 misattributes supplier manufacturing to direct operations.", "type": "single", "options": ["S1: Scope 1; S2: Scope 2; S3: Scope 2; S4: Scope 3; S5: Scope 3.", "S1: Scope 2; S2: Scope 1; S3: Scope 2; S4: Scope 3; S5: Scope 3.", "S1: Scope 1; S2: Scope 2; S3: Scope 1; S4: Scope 2; S5: Scope 3.", "S1: Scope 3; S2: Scope 2; S3: Scope 2; S4: Scope 1; S5: Scope 1."], "answer": 0, "hints": ["Think about: classify AI system emissions sources into Scope 1, Scope 2, and Scope 3 under the GHG Protocol"]}, {"q": "A systems team aggressively compresses a model using unstructured pruning to cut training FLOPs by 60 percent. In production, serving the sparse model requires specialized, custom-fabricated NPU accelerators and requires double the serving iterations to reach quality parity. Which pitfall does this scenario illustrate, and what is the proper engineering mitigation?", "explanation": "The correct answer is A. Local optimization of training compute without full-lifecycle assessment; the mitigation is conducting end-to-end Life Cycle Assessment (LCA) that accounts for hardware manufacturing, deployment scale, and serving energy before committing to architectural changes. Optimizing one isolated phase (training FLOPs) often shifts environmental burdens to other lifecycle phases, such as requiring new specialized hardware fabrication (embodied carbon) or increasing recurring inference queries. Comprehensive LCA across training, serving, and hardware manufacturing prevents counterproductive local optimizations. Amdahl's law does not address lifecycle trade-offs; reclassifying emissions scopes does not alter physical carbon; and workstation cooling is irrelevant to datacenter-scale deployment.", "type": "single", "options": ["Local optimization of training compute without full-lifecycle assessment; the mitigation is conducting end-to-end Life Cycle Assessment (LCA) that accounts for hardware manufacturing, deployment scale, and serving energy before committing to architectural changes.", "Failure to apply Amdahl's law; the mitigation is increasing CPU clock frequency.", "Violating the GHG Protocol; the mitigation is reclassifying Scope 3 emissions as Scope 1.", "Ignoring PUE; the mitigation is installing liquid cooling on the development workstations."], "answer": 0, "hints": ["Think about: analyze the pitfalls of localized optimization and apply full Life Cycle Assessment to evaluate net environmental impact"]}, {"q": "The metric representing the mass of carbon dioxide emitted per unit of electricity generated, expressed in grams of $\\text{CO}_2$ per kilowatt-hour, is known as `____`.", "explanation": "carbon intensity. Grid carbon intensity ($\\text{gCO}_2/\\text{kWh}$) varies by location and time, governing the operational emissions associated with electricity consumption.", "type": "fill", "answer": ["carbon intensity"], "hints": ["Think about: explain how grid carbon intensity translates electrical energy into operational carbon emissions"]}, {"q": "Give one valid order for the following steps in computing the specified operational-plus-accelerator-manufacturing carbon subtotal of a model training workload:", "explanation": "One valid order is: (3) measure or estimate raw IT electrical power and duration across GPUs, CPUs, and memory, (1) multiply IT energy by facility PUE to obtain total facility energy consumption, (5) multiply total facility energy by regional grid carbon intensity to determine operational carbon, (2) amortize accelerator manufacturing footprint over total operational lifetime to calculate the job's embodied share, (4) sum operational and embodied carbon to determine the accounted subtotal. IT energy must be measured first; facility PUE is applied to calculate total electrical draw; grid carbon intensity converts facility energy into operational emissions; embodied manufacturing carbon is amortized over the run's duration; and both components are summed for this partial lifecycle account. Manufacturing of hosts, networking, storage, and facilities, and any other lifecycle stages outside the stated inventory, must be added before claiming a complete footprint. PUE and carbon intensity may be multiplied in either order, but facility overhead must be included exactly once; embodied carbon can be computed independently before the final sum.", "type": "order", "items": ["measure or estimate raw IT electrical power and duration across GPUs, CPUs, and memory", "multiply IT energy by facility PUE to obtain total facility energy consumption", "multiply total facility energy by regional grid carbon intensity to determine operational carbon.", "amortize accelerator manufacturing footprint over total operational lifetime to calculate the job's embodied share", "sum operational and embodied carbon to determine the accounted subtotal"], "hints": ["Think about: analyze the sequential calculation workflow for AI lifecycle carbon accounting"]}, {"q": "If an AI training data center is powered 100 percent by dedicated geothermal and hydroelectric power, the use phase is completely unconstrained and engineers no longer need to consider grid dynamics or cooling efficiency.", "explanation": "False. Low-carbon sourcing does not remove electrical capacity, thermal, water, or reliability limits, and it does not erase generation or hardware lifecycle burdens. Any effect on other grid generation depends on the actual supply and grid connection; dedicated hydro or geothermal alone does not establish a peaker response.", "type": "truefalse", "answer": false, "hints": ["Think about: evaluate how zero-carbon electricity sourcing changes emissions accounting while physical use-phase constraints remain binding"]}, {"q": "An engineer needs to profile the energy consumption of a sub-watt microcontroller executing an on-device wake-word detection model that spends 98 percent of its time in deep sleep drawing 30 $\\mu\\text{W}$. The microcontroller lacks on-chip energy counters. Which measurement approach is appropriate?", "explanation": "The correct answer is D. An external analyzer must resolve both sleep and active states at the declared electrical boundary. Its configured range, bandwidth, calibration, voltage measurement, and integration interval determine whether it can do so; a fast sample rate or product name alone is insufficient. RAPL and NVML do not supply this microcontroller measurement, and parameter count omits duty-cycle and peripheral costs.", "type": "single", "options": ["Sampling the Linux RAPL interface at 100 Hz to capture CPU package power states.", "Querying NVML/nvidia-smi programmatic bindings to integrate instantaneous milliwatt metrics.", "Multiplying model parameter count by a theoretical constant of 5 pJ per parameter.", "Using a calibrated external analyzer with verified current range, analog bandwidth, synchronized voltage measurement, and integration windows covering sleep, active execution, and transitions."], "answer": 3, "hints": ["Think about: select instrumentation capable of measuring the declared duty cycle and electrical boundary of a TinyML system"]}], "mode": "test"}], "id": "v2-15-99-test", "chapter": "v2-15", "minutes": 10}});
